// Full stories: Cosmos and Earth & Life.
window.STORY6=Object.assign(window.STORY6||{},{
"First atoms & the cosmic background":["For its first 380,000 years the universe was a glowing fog of charged particles that trapped light. When it cooled to about 3,000 °C, electrons joined nuclei to make the first atoms, and light could at last travel freely.","That light, stretched by cosmic expansion into microwaves, fills the whole sky today. Its tiny temperature ripples are the seeds from which galaxies grew."],
"First stars":["After the first light faded, the universe went through its 'dark ages', with no stars at all. Gravity slowly pulled clumps of hydrogen and helium together until, perhaps 100 to 200 million years after the Big Bang, the first stars ignited.","These stars were probably huge and short-lived. When they exploded they forged the first heavier elements, such as carbon and oxygen, which later went into planets and living things."],
"The Milky Way begins":["Our galaxy's oldest stars are almost as old as the universe. The Milky Way grew by drawing in gas and swallowing smaller galaxies, and it is still doing so.","Today it holds a few hundred billion stars in a disc about 100,000 light years across, with a black hole four million times the Sun's mass at its centre. Many cultures saw it as a river: the Silver River in China, the Ganga of the sky in India, the Path of Souls for many Native American peoples."],
"Cosmic noon & a galactic merger":["About 10 billion years ago the universe was forming stars faster than at any time before or since. Astronomers call it 'cosmic noon'.","Around then the Milky Way collided with a smaller galaxy, now called Gaia-Enceladus. The European Gaia space telescope found its scattered stars in 2018, still moving in the strange orbits they had before the merger."],
"Heavy bombardment?":["Moon rocks brought back by Apollo astronauts gave many dates close to 3.9 billion years ago, suggesting a sudden storm of impacts on the inner planets, the 'Late Heavy Bombardment'.","Many scientists now think the rocks came mostly from one or a few big basins, and that impacts tailed off gradually instead. The question matters because heavy impacts could have sterilized or seeded early life."],
"Supernova of 1054":["On 4 July 1054, Chinese court astronomers recorded a 'guest star' near the constellation Taurus, visible by day for 23 days and at night for nearly two years. Japanese and Arab observers also noted it.","Its remains are the Crab Nebula, with a spinning neutron star at its heart. A rock painting at Chaco Canyon in New Mexico, showing a star beside a crescent moon, may record the same event."],
"Tycho's supernova":["In November 1572, the Danish astronomer Tycho Brahe saw a brilliant new star in Cassiopeia. He measured it carefully and showed it lay far beyond the Moon.","This contradicted the old teaching that the heavens never change. Korean and Chinese astronomers also recorded it."],
"The universe is expanding":["In 1927 the Belgian priest and physicist Georges Lemaître argued that the universe is expanding. In 1929 Edwin Hubble, at Mount Wilson in California, showed that the farther a galaxy is, the faster it is moving away.","Running the expansion backwards led to the idea of the Big Bang. Hubble used distances measured with a method found by Henrietta Swan Leavitt, who studied variable stars."],
"Echo of the Big Bang found":["In 1964 and 1965, Arno Penzias and Robert Wilson at Bell Labs in New Jersey found a faint hiss in their antenna that came from every direction. They cleaned out pigeon droppings, but the noise stayed.","Nearby, physicists at Princeton realized it was the afterglow of the Big Bang they had been planning to look for. Penzias and Wilson won the Nobel Prize."],
"Comet hits Jupiter":["Comet Shoemaker-Levy 9 had been torn into over twenty pieces by Jupiter's gravity. In July 1994 they hit Jupiter one after another.","The impacts left dark scars larger than Earth. It was the first collision between solar-system bodies ever observed, and showed the danger of impacts."],
"First planet around a Sun-like star":["In 1995 Michel Mayor and Didier Queloz in Geneva found a planet circling the star 51 Pegasi, by detecting the star's tiny wobble. It was a giant planet orbiting closer than Mercury, in just four days.","No one had expected such planets. Over 6,000 exoplanets are now known, and Mayor and Queloz won the Nobel Prize in 2019."],
"Voyager 1 enters interstellar space":["In August 2012, Voyager 1, launched in 1977, crossed the heliopause, where the Sun's wind gives way to the gas between the stars.","It still sends weak signals from over 24 billion kilometres away; a message takes almost a day to reach it."],
"Gravitational waves detected":["On 14 September 2015, the LIGO detectors in Washington and Louisiana registered a tiny ripple in space itself, made by two black holes merging over a billion years ago.","Einstein had predicted such waves in 1916 but doubted they could ever be detected. The ripple changed the length of LIGO's 4-kilometre arms by less than the width of a proton."],
"First image of a black hole":["In April 2019 the Event Horizon Telescope, linking radio dishes across the world into one Earth-sized telescope, released the first image of a black hole: a glowing ring around a dark centre in the galaxy M87.","The black hole is 6.5 billion times the Sun's mass. Its Hawaiian name, Pōwehi, given by a Hawaiian language professor, means 'embellished dark source of unending creation'."],
"Webb's first deep field":["On 11 July 2022, the first full-colour image from the James Webb Space Telescope showed thousands of galaxies behind the cluster SMACS 0723, in a patch of sky the size of a grain of sand held at arm's length.","Some of the light had travelled for over 13 billion years."],
"3I/ATLAS":["On 1 July 2025, the ATLAS survey telescope in Chile spotted a comet moving too fast to be bound to the Sun. It was only the third object known to come from another star system, after 'Oumuamua in 2017 and Borisov in 2019.","Telescopes and spacecraft studied it as it passed through the inner solar system; it may be older than the Sun."],
"Total solar eclipse over Europe":["On 12 August 2026 the Moon's shadow crossed Greenland, Iceland and northern Spain, giving the first total eclipse in mainland Europe since 1999.","Iceland and Spain prepared for large numbers of eclipse travellers. Spain will see two more total or annular eclipses in 2027 and 2028."],
"The Moon forms":["The leading theory is that about 4.5 billion years ago a Mars-sized body, named Theia, struck the young Earth. Debris thrown into orbit gathered into the Moon.","Moon rocks are chemically almost identical to Earth's mantle, supporting the idea. The Moon steadies Earth's tilt and drives the tides."],
"Oldest minerals":["Tiny zircon crystals from the Jack Hills in Western Australia are up to 4.4 billion years old, the oldest known pieces of Earth.","Their oxygen chemistry suggests they formed in contact with water, meaning the young Earth cooled quickly and may have had oceans soon after it formed."],
"Great Oxidation Event":["About 2.4 billion years ago, oxygen made by cyanobacteria began to build up in the air. Iron in the oceans rusted out, leaving banded iron formations, mined today for steel.","For many microbes oxygen was poison. The change may also have triggered a long global ice age."],
"Complex cells":["About 1.8 billion years ago, eukaryotes appeared: cells with a nucleus and internal parts.","Their mitochondria were once free-living bacteria swallowed by another cell, an idea championed by Lynn Margulis. All plants, animals and fungi descend from these cells."],
"Snowball Earth":["Between about 720 and 635 million years ago, ice may have reached the equator. Glacial rocks from that time are found in places that were then tropical.","Volcanic carbon dioxide eventually warmed the planet. Soon after, the first large animals appeared."],
"Ediacaran life":["About 570 million years ago, strange soft-bodied creatures lived on the sea floor: quilted fronds, discs and flat ovals such as Dickinsonia, now known to be an animal.","They are named after the Ediacara Hills in South Australia. Most vanished before the Cambrian."],
"Plants on land":["Around 470 million years ago, small plants related to liverworts began to grow on land, with fungi helping them draw nutrients from the rocks.","They made soil, changed the air, and opened the land to animals."],
"Mass extinction 1: Ordovician":["About 445 million years ago, a sudden ice age locked water into glaciers, dropping sea levels and cooling the oceans.","About 85 percent of marine species died, including many trilobites and brachiopods. It was the first of the 'Big Five' extinctions."],
"Fish walk onto land":["In 2004 Neil Shubin's team found Tiktaalik in Canada's Arctic, a 375-million-year-old fish with a neck, lungs and sturdy fins with wrist bones.","Fish like it gave rise to the first four-limbed animals. Every land vertebrate, including us, descends from them; the Inuit elders who advised the team chose its name, meaning a large freshwater fish."],
"Mass extinction 2: Devonian":["Around 372 million years ago, a series of crises, probably including oxygen loss in the oceans, killed many marine species.","The great reefs, built by corals and sponges, collapsed and did not recover for millions of years."],
"First reptiles":["About 312 million years ago, animals such as Hylonomus, found in fossil tree stumps at Joggins, Nova Scotia, laid eggs with protective membranes.","Freed from returning to water to breed, they spread inland. Reptiles, birds and mammals descend from them."],
"Coal forests & Pangaea":["Around 300 million years ago, vast swamp forests of giant clubmosses and ferns covered the tropics. Their buried remains became most of the world's coal.","Oxygen levels rose high enough for dragonflies with wings over 60 cm across. The continents joined into the single supercontinent Pangaea."],
"First dinosaurs":["The earliest known dinosaurs, such as Eoraptor and Herrerasaurus from Argentina, lived about 230 million years ago. They were small to medium-sized and walked on two legs.","For their first 30 million years they were a minor group. After the end-Triassic extinction, they came to dominate the land."],
"First mammals":["Mammals evolved from earlier animals called cynodonts. The first true mammals, such as Morganucodon, were small, shrew-like, probably nocturnal, and nursed their young.","For over 150 million years they lived in the dinosaurs' shadow."],
"Mass extinction 4: Triassic":["About 201 million years ago, volcanic eruptions as the supercontinent Pangaea began to split released huge amounts of carbon dioxide.","About three-quarters of species died. Dinosaurs survived and spread."],
"Archaeopteryx":["In 1861, two years after Darwin's Origin of Species, quarry workers in Bavaria found Archaeopteryx: a crow-sized animal with feathers, wings, teeth and a long bony tail.","It showed that birds evolved from dinosaurs. Since the 1990s, feathered dinosaurs from China have confirmed it."],
"Flowering plants":["Flowering plants appear about 130 million years ago. Darwin called their sudden rise an 'abominable mystery'.","They spread rapidly with insects as partners. Today they make up about 90 percent of plant species, and nearly all human food."],
"Hothouse Earth":["About 56 million years ago, a huge release of carbon, possibly from volcanoes and seabed methane, warmed the planet by 5 to 8 °C over a few thousand years.","Oceans acidified, and palm trees grew near the Arctic. Today's carbon emissions are happening at least ten times faster."],
"Antarctica freezes":["About 34 million years ago, carbon dioxide fell and Antarctica separated from South America, letting a cold current circle it. Ice sheets formed.","The ice still holds enough water to raise sea levels by about 58 metres."],
"The Ice Ages begin":["About 2.58 million years ago, ice sheets began growing and shrinking in cycles, driven by changes in Earth's orbit.","Humans evolved through these cycles. We live in a warm interval."],
"Last 'hobbits' of Flores":["Homo floresiensis, a human species about a metre tall, lived on the Indonesian island of Flores. Its remains were found in Liang Bua cave in 2003.","It vanished about 50,000 years ago, around when modern humans reached the region. The Nage people of Flores tell stories of small hairy people called Ebu Gogo."],
"Campanian eruption":["About 39,900 years ago, a huge eruption at the Campi Flegrei near Naples spread ash across Europe and western Asia.","It cooled the climate. Some think it hastened the Neanderthals' end."],
"Doggerland drowns":["When sea levels were lower, Doggerland joined Britain to Europe, with hills, rivers and hunters. Fishing boats still drag up mammoth bones and stone tools.","As seas rose, it shrank. Around 6200 BCE the Storegga tsunami, caused by a landslide off Norway, struck its last islands."],
"Green Sahara dries":["From about 9000 to 3500 BCE, monsoon rains made the Sahara a land of lakes and grassland. Rock paintings at Tassili n'Ajjer show cattle, giraffes and swimmers.","As rains failed, people moved to the Nile and other refuges, perhaps feeding the rise of Egypt."],
"About 250 million people":["Around the turn of the eras the world held roughly 250 million people, about as many as live in Indonesia today. Estimates rest on Roman and Han census records and guesses for everywhere else.", "The Han census of 2 CE counted about 57 million people, and the Roman Empire held perhaps 50 to 60 million. Most people everywhere were farmers, and about half of children died before adulthood."],
"Medieval Warm Period":["From about 950 to 1250, parts of the North Atlantic were warmer than in the centuries before or after. Norse settlers farmed in Greenland, and vineyards grew in England.", "It was not warm everywhere: the American Southwest suffered megadroughts, which may have helped drive Ancestral Puebloan people from Chaco Canyon and Mesa Verde."],
"Little Ice Age":["From about 1300 to 1850, the Northern Hemisphere was cooler. Alpine glaciers swallowed villages, and the frozen Thames held 'frost fairs' with stalls and ice skating.", "Cold years brought famines in Europe, China and elsewhere. Volcanic eruptions and low solar activity were likely causes; one theory adds forest regrowth in the Americas after Indigenous populations collapsed."],
"Shaanxi earthquake":["On 23 January 1556, an earthquake struck Shaanxi province in China. Many people lived in caves cut into soft loess cliffs, which collapsed.","About 830,000 people died, the deadliest earthquake recorded. The scholar Qin Keda, who survived, advised people to run outside at the first tremor."],
"Last aurochs":["The aurochs, huge wild cattle with long horns, once roamed Europe, Asia and North Africa, and were painted at Lascaux.","The last known one, a cow, died in 1627 in the Jaktorów forest in Poland, despite royal protection."],
"Carrington Event":["On 1 September 1859, astronomer Richard Carrington saw a bright flash on the Sun. Within a day, a solar storm struck Earth.","Auroras lit skies as far south as the Caribbean, and telegraph wires sparked. A storm like it today could damage power grids and satellites."],
"Yellow River flood":["In September 1887 the Yellow River broke its dykes at Zhengzhou in Henan, flooding some 130,000 square kilometres of the North China Plain.", "Perhaps 900,000 or more people died from drowning, famine and disease. The river's silt raises its bed above the surrounding plain, which is why it has been called 'China's sorrow'."],
"Mount Pelée":["In spring 1902, Mount Pelée on Martinique rumbled for weeks, but officials, preparing for an election, told people Saint-Pierre was safe. On 8 May a glowing cloud of gas and ash swept over the town in about a minute.", "About 28,000 people died. One of very few survivors, the prisoner Ludger Sylbaris, was shielded in a thick-walled cell and later toured with a circus."],
"San Francisco earthquake":["At 5:12 on the morning of 18 April 1906, an earthquake of about magnitude 7.9 ruptured the San Andreas Fault. Broken gas lines started fires that burned for three days.", "Around 3,000 people died and over half the city's population lost their homes. Chinatown was destroyed, and city leaders tried, and failed, to move its residents out of the city centre."],
"Tunguska event":["On the morning of 30 June 1908, an asteroid or comet fragment exploded several kilometres above the Podkamennaya Tunguska River in Siberia, flattening about 80 million trees over 2,000 square kilometres.", "Evenki reindeer herders described a blinding light and a blast that threw people to the ground; a trading post 65 km away felt searing heat. Because it hit no crater, the first expedition only reached the site in 1927."],
"2 billion people":["The world's population reached about two billion around 1927, roughly 120 years after it reached one billion.", "Better food supplies, clean water, vaccination and falling child deaths drove the growth. It took only 33 years more to reach three billion."],
"China floods":["In the summer of 1931, heavy rains after a long drought swelled the Yangtze, Huai and Yellow rivers. Floodwater covered an area the size of England, and the city of Wuhan stood underwater for months.", "Estimates of the dead range from hundreds of thousands to several million, many from cholera and famine after the water receded. The Nationalist government organized relief with international aid."],
"Dust Bowl":["In the 1930s, drought struck the southern Great Plains of the United States, where millions of acres of grassland had been ploughed for wheat. With no roots to hold it, the soil blew away in 'black blizzards'.", "About 2.5 million people left the plains, many for California, where they were called 'Okies'. The government began paying farmers to protect their soil."],
"Valdivia earthquake":["On 22 May 1960, a magnitude 9.5 earthquake, the strongest ever recorded, struck southern Chile. The ground in places dropped two metres.", "Its tsunami killed people in Hawaiʻi, Japan and the Philippines, hours later. Mapuche communities in the area carried out a ritual to calm the sea, a sign of the trauma."],
"Bhola cyclone":["On the night of 12 November 1970, a cyclone drove a storm surge over the low islands of East Pakistan's Ganges delta. Up to 500,000 people died, the deadliest tropical cyclone on record.", "Bengalis were angered by the West Pakistan government's slow response. Weeks later the Awami League won a landslide election, the road to Bangladesh's independence war."],
"Smallpox eradicated":["Smallpox killed perhaps 300 million people in the 20th century. A global vaccination campaign, led by the WHO, traced and contained outbreaks.","The last natural case was Ali Maow Maalin in Somalia in 1977. In 1980 the WHO declared smallpox eradicated."],
"Haiti earthquake":["On 12 January 2010, a magnitude 7.0 earthquake struck near Port-au-Prince, Haiti's capital, where many buildings were not built to withstand quakes.", "Estimates of deaths range from about 100,000 to over 300,000, and over a million people were left homeless. Months later, UN peacekeepers introduced cholera, which killed nearly 10,000 people."],
"Turkey-Syria earthquakes":["Before dawn on 6 February 2023, a magnitude 7.8 earthquake struck southern Turkey near the Syrian border, followed hours later by a magnitude 7.5 quake.", "Over 50,000 people died in Turkey and thousands more in Syria, where years of war hampered rescue. Many buildings that collapsed had been built in breach of building codes."],
"Afghanistan earthquake":["On the night of 31 August 2025, a shallow magnitude 6.0 earthquake struck Kunar and Nangarhar provinces in eastern Afghanistan.", "About 2,200 people died, many in mud-brick houses in remote mountain valleys that rescuers struggled to reach. Aid cuts left relief agencies short of funds."],
"Renewables overtake coal":["In 2025, renewables (solar, wind, hydro and others) generated about 34 percent of the world's electricity, overtaking coal for the first time since 1919, according to the energy think tank Ember.", "Solar power grew faster than any electricity source in history, with China the largest driver. The International Energy Agency's figures showed the two almost tied."]
});
// Full stories: Africa.
window.STORY6=Object.assign(window.STORY6||{},{
"Mitochondrial Eve":["Mitochondria, the energy factories in our cells, are passed down only from mothers. Tracing their DNA back, every living person shares one female ancestor through the mother's line, who lived in Africa roughly 150,000 to 200,000 years ago.","She was not the only woman alive, nor the first human. Other women of her time also left descendants, but not through an unbroken line of daughters."],
"Blombos Cave engravings":["In Blombos Cave on South Africa's southern coast, people about 75,000 years ago scratched cross-hatched patterns onto pieces of red ochre. The same cave held shell beads and a drawing in ochre crayon on a stone flake.","These are among the oldest known signs of symbolic thought: marks made to mean something, not just to be useful."],
"Green Sahara rock art":["On the plateau of Tassili n'Ajjer in Algeria, thousands of paintings and engravings show the Sahara when it was green: herds of cattle, giraffes, hippos, people swimming, dancing and herding.","The earliest show wild animals and 'round-headed' figures; later ones show cattle herders, and later still horses and camels, a picture record of the desert's drying. Tuareg people live in the region today."],
"Nabta Playa stones":["At Nabta Playa in Egypt's Western Desert, cattle herders around 5000 BCE set up a small circle of upright stones and lines of standing stones.","Two pairs of stones may line up with sunrise at the summer solstice, when the rains came. Herders there also buried cattle in stone-lined tombs, perhaps foreshadowing later Egyptian cattle cults."],
"Papyrus":["Egyptians made a writing material by laying strips of papyrus reed in two crossed layers and pressing them until the plant's sap glued them together. A blank roll was found in the tomb of the official Hemaka, about 2900 BCE.","Papyrus was Egypt's great export, used by Greeks and Romans for scrolls. The words 'paper' and 'Bible' (from the Phoenician papyrus port of Byblos) come from it."],
"365-day calendar":["Egyptians used a civil year of twelve months of thirty days, plus five extra days at the end, celebrated as the birthdays of the gods Osiris, Horus, Set, Isis and Nephthys.","The year slowly slipped against the seasons, because it lacked a leap day. Julius Caesar's calendar, drawn up with help from the Alexandrian astronomer Sosigenes, added one, and our calendar descends from it."],
"Early dynasties of Egypt":["Egypt's first kings were buried at Abydos in Upper Egypt, with great funerary enclosures, while officials built tombs at Saqqara near the capital Memphis.","In the first dynasties the kings developed writing, taxation, royal titles and the idea of the pharaoh as the god Horus on Earth."],
"Step Pyramid of Djoser":["Around 2630 BCE, the architect Imhotep built a tomb for King Djoser at Saqqara by stacking six stone platforms, each smaller than the one below. It is the world's oldest large stone building.","The complex around it imitated in stone the reed and wood buildings of earlier times. Egyptians later honoured Imhotep as a sage and, eventually, a god."],
"Kerma, Nubia":["Around 2500 BCE, the kingdom of Kerma arose on the Nile in what is now Sudan, Egypt's first great African neighbour to the south.","Its people built a huge mud-brick temple, the Deffufa, which still stands about 18 metres tall. They are known only through archaeology and Egyptian records, which call the land Kush."],
"Pepi II":["Pepi II became king of Egypt as a boy of about six. A letter he sent to the explorer Harkhuf, carved on Harkhuf's tomb, shows him excited about a dancing pygmy being brought back from the south, urging that he be guarded day and night.","Tradition says he reigned for 94 years, which would be the longest in history; some scholars think it was closer to 64. Egypt's Old Kingdom fell apart soon after his death."],
"Middle Kingdom":["Around 2055 BCE, Mentuhotep II of Thebes reunited Egypt after a period of division. His temple at Deir el-Bahri was later the model for Hatshepsut's.","Middle Kingdom Egyptians wrote many of their greatest literary works and later saw it as a classical age. Pharaohs pushed south into Nubia and built huge forts there."],
"The Shipwrecked Sailor":["An Egyptian papyrus of about 1950 BCE tells of a sailor whose ship sank in a storm. Washed up on an island, he meets a giant golden serpent with eyebrows of lapis lazuli, the lord of Punt.","The serpent tells him of its own sorrow, the loss of its family, and sends him home with gifts. It is one of the world's oldest surviving stories."],
"Story of Sinuhe":["The Story of Sinuhe tells of a courtier who panics when King Amenemhat I is assassinated and flees to Syria. He prospers there among foreign tribes, but longs to be buried in Egypt.","In old age he receives a letter from the new king inviting him home. Egyptians considered it their finest story, and copied it for 800 years."],
"Moscow Mathematical Papyrus":["This papyrus, written around 1850 BCE and now in Moscow, contains 25 worked problems. One correctly finds the volume of a truncated pyramid, a result not equalled in Greece until much later.","Others calculate the strength of beer and bread and the area of a curved surface."],
"Kerma at its height":["By about 1750 BCE, Kerma controlled much of the Nile from the third to the fifth cataract, rivalling Egypt.","Its kings were buried under huge mounds with hundreds of human sacrifices. An Egyptian inscription at El Kab, found in 2003, records a Kushite army raiding as far as Upper Egypt."],
"Hyksos rule Egypt":["Around 1650 BCE, rulers of Levantine origin, called Hyksos, 'rulers of foreign lands', took power in northern Egypt from their capital Avaris.","They brought horses, chariots and new bronze weapons. Egyptians drove them out around 1550 BCE and later wrote of them as hated invaders; archaeology suggests they ruled more like Egyptian kings."],
"Edwin Smith Papyrus":["This medical papyrus of about 1600 BCE describes 48 cases of injuries, from head wounds to broken bones, starting at the head and working down.","For each it gives an examination, a diagnosis, and a verdict: treatable, uncertain, or not treatable. It describes the brain and its effect on the body, and relies mostly on practical treatment rather than spells."],
"Book of the Dead":["Egyptians called it the 'Book of Coming Forth by Day'. These spells, written on papyrus and placed in tombs, helped the dead travel through the underworld.","In the most famous scene, the dead person's heart is weighed against the feather of Maat, truth. If the heart was heavy with wrongdoing, the monster Ammit devoured it."],
"Rhind & Ebers papyri":["The Rhind Papyrus, copied by the scribe Ahmose around 1550 BCE, teaches fractions, areas and volumes through 84 problems, and gives a value for pi of about 3.16.","The Ebers Papyrus records about 700 remedies, including treatments for asthma, tumours and depression, alongside spells."],
"Expedition to Punt":["Around 1470 BCE Hatshepsut sent five ships to the land of Punt. Reliefs at her temple show sailors loading incense trees, ebony, gold and baboons, and the Puntite king Parahu and his queen Ati.","Punt was probably in the region of Eritrea and Somalia; analysis of mummified baboons supports that."],
"Atenism vanishes":["After Akhenaten's death, his young son Tutankhaten changed his name to Tutankhamun, honouring the god Amun, and the court left Amarna.","Later kings chiselled Akhenaten's name and images off monuments and called him 'that criminal'. The rediscovery of Amarna in the 1800s revealed his reign."],
"Tutankhamun dies":["Tutankhamun became king at about nine and died at about nineteen, possibly from an infection after a broken leg; scans show he also had malaria.","In 1922 Howard Carter, working with Egyptian labourers including the boy Hussein Abdel-Rassoul, found his tomb nearly intact, with over 5,000 objects."],
"Sekhmet & the Eye of Ra":["In the Book of the Heavenly Cow, the sun god Ra grows old and humans plot against him. He sends his Eye as Sekhmet, the lioness, who slaughters people and delights in their blood.","To stop her, Ra floods the fields with beer dyed red with ochre. She drinks, becomes drunk, and spares the rest of humanity. Egyptians celebrated with drinking festivals."],
"Ramesses II":["Ramesses II ruled for 66 years and fathered over 100 children. He built Abu Simbel, the Ramesseum, and much of Karnak and Luxor.","He proclaimed the Battle of Kadesh against the Hittites a great victory, though it was a draw. He then made the earliest surviving peace treaty with them."],
"Merneptah Stele":["Ramesses II's son Merneptah set up a stone about 1208 BCE celebrating his victories. One line reads: 'Israel is laid waste, its seed is not'.","It is the first known mention of Israel, as a people in Canaan."],
"End of the New Kingdom":["Around 1070 BCE Egypt split: the high priests of Amun ruled Thebes, while kings ruled from Tanis in the north.","The Tomb Robbery Papyri show how, as government weakened, royal tombs were plundered. Priests reburied the royal mummies in hidden caches."],
"Queen of Sheba":["In the Bible, the Queen of Sheba visits King Solomon with gold and spices to test his wisdom. The Qur'an tells a related story of a queen of Saba, called Bilqis in Islamic tradition.","Ethiopia's Kebra Nagast, 'Glory of the Kings', written down in the 1300s, says her name was Makeda, and her son by Solomon, Menelik I, founded Ethiopia's royal line and brought the Ark of the Covenant to Aksum. Yemen also claims her."],
"Imhotep becomes a god":["By the Late Period, Egyptians worshipped Imhotep, architect of the Step Pyramid, as a son of the god Ptah and a god of healing.","Greeks identified him with Asclepius. The sick visited his shrine at Saqqara."],
"Hanno's voyage":["A Greek text claims to translate an inscription set up in Carthage by the admiral Hanno, who sailed down Africa's Atlantic coast with sixty ships around 500 BCE.","He describes rivers, burning lands and hairy 'gorillas' whom his men captured. How far he went is debated."],
"Euclid's Elements":["Around 300 BCE in Alexandria, Euclid assembled the Elements, thirteen books building geometry from a few definitions and axioms.","It was copied, translated into Arabic and Latin, and used as a textbook for over 2,000 years."],
"Lighthouse of Alexandria":["Built around 280 BCE on the island of Pharos, the lighthouse may have been over 100 metres tall, with a fire at the top.","Earthquakes damaged it, and by 1480 the Mamluk sultan Qaitbay built a fort from its stones. Divers have found its remains."],
"Djenné-Djenno":["Djenné-Djenno, near Djenné in Mali's inland Niger delta, was a town by about 250 BCE, one of the oldest in sub-Saharan Africa.","Its people farmed rice, worked iron and traded, without a palace or obvious ruler. It was abandoned around 1400."],
"Eratosthenes measures Earth":["Eratosthenes, librarian at Alexandria, learned that at noon on the summer solstice the Sun shone straight down a well at Syene (Aswan). At Alexandria, at the same time, shadows fell at about 7 degrees.","From this and the distance between the cities he calculated Earth's circumference, probably within a few percent."],
"Rosetta Stone":["In 196 BCE, Egyptian priests issued a decree honouring the young king Ptolemy V, carved in hieroglyphs, demotic and Greek.","French soldiers found it in 1799; it passed to Britain in 1801. Egypt has asked for its return."],
"Death of Cleopatra":["Cleopatra VII, the last Ptolemaic ruler and the first to learn Egyptian, allied with Julius Caesar and then Mark Antony.","After their defeat, she died in 30 BCE, by tradition from a snakebite. Her story is known only from Roman writers."],
"Hero's steam engine":["Hero of Alexandria, an engineer and teacher, described the aeolipile: a hollow ball on an axle, spun by jets of steam escaping from two bent nozzles.","His books also describe temple doors that opened when a fire was lit, a coin-operated holy water dispenser, and a programmable cart driven by falling weights. No one in the ancient world used steam to do real work."],
"Ptolemy's Almagest":["Around 150 CE, Claudius Ptolemy of Alexandria wrote a great mathematical model of the heavens, with Earth at the centre and planets moving on circles upon circles, and a catalogue of over 1,000 stars.","Arab astronomers called it al-Majisti, 'the greatest', which became 'Almagest'. They tested and corrected it for centuries, and it guided astronomy until Copernicus and Kepler."],
"Septimius Severus":["Born at Leptis Magna in today's Libya to a family of Punic descent, Septimius Severus seized power in Rome in 193 after a year of civil war.","He rebuilt Leptis Magna in marble, with a great arch and forum that still stand. On his deathbed at York in 211 he is said to have told his sons to stay united, enrich the soldiers, and ignore everyone else."],
"Desert Fathers":["Around 270, a young Egyptian named Anthony heard the Gospel command to sell all he had and give it to the poor. He went into the desert to live alone in prayer, and thousands followed.","Pachomius, a former soldier, organized monks into communities under a common rule. Their sayings, collected in Greek and Coptic, shaped Christian monasticism everywhere."],
"Aksum ends Meroë":["Around 350, King Ezana of Aksum marched to the Nile. His inscription in Ge'ez records defeating the Noba and burning their towns and the cities of the Kasu.","The ancient kingdom of Kush, already weakened, faded away, and new Nubian kingdoms arose in its place."],
"Hypatia killed":["Hypatia was Alexandria's leading mathematician and philosopher, who taught both pagan and Christian students and edited works on astronomy and geometry.","In March 415, caught up in a power struggle between the bishop Cyril and the governor Orestes, she was seized by a Christian mob, dragged into a church and killed. The church historian Socrates Scholasticus condemned the murder."],
"Vandals take North Africa":["In 429 the Vandal king Gaiseric led about 80,000 people across from Spain into North Africa. Saint Augustine died in 430 while they besieged his city of Hippo.","They took Carthage in 439 and built a powerful fleet; in 455 they sacked Rome for two weeks. A Byzantine army reconquered the region in 533 and 534."],
"Last temple of Isis closes":["The temple of Isis at Philae, on an island near the first cataract, stayed open long after others closed, because Rome had agreed that Nubian peoples could worship there.","The last hieroglyphic inscription anywhere was carved at Philae in 394. Around 537 Justinian ordered the temple closed and its statues taken to Constantinople. In the 1970s the temple was moved stone by stone to save it from the rising waters of the Aswan dam."],
"The Baqt treaty":["In 652 an Arab army besieged Dongola, capital of the Nubian kingdom of Makuria. Nubian archers were so accurate that Arab sources called them 'pupil-smiters'.","Unable to win, the two sides agreed the baqt: each year Nubia sent enslaved people, and Egypt sent grain, wine and cloth. The arrangement kept Christian Nubia independent for about 600 years."],
"Kanem Empire":["Around 700, the Kanem Empire arose northeast of Lake Chad, built on horses, trade and control of Saharan routes. Its Sefuwa kings converted to Islam in the late 1000s.","The dynasty later moved west of the lake to Bornu. As Kanem-Bornu it lasted over a thousand years, until the 1800s. Its king-list, the Diwan, is one of Africa's oldest written records."],
"Sijilmasa":["Founded in 757 in an oasis of southern Morocco, Sijilmasa was the northern gateway of the trans-Saharan trade.","Caravans of thousands of camels carried West African gold north, where much of it was minted into coins used across the Mediterranean, and carried salt, cloth and books south. Its ruins lie near Rissani."],
"Wagadu & the serpent Bida":["Soninke legend tells that the serpent Bida, guardian of Wagadu, rained gold on the land in return for the most beautiful maiden each year. When the maiden Sia Yatabéré was chosen, her lover Mamadi Sefe Dekote cut off the serpent's head.","Its dying curse brought drought and the end of the gold rain, and the Soninke clans scattered. The legend, kept by griots, explains the fall of Ghana."],
"University of al-Qarawiyyin":["In 859 Fatima al-Fihri, the daughter of a wealthy merchant from Kairouan who had settled in Fez, used her inheritance to build the Qarawiyyin mosque.","It grew into a great centre of learning in law, theology, grammar, mathematics and astronomy. UNESCO and Guinness call it the oldest university still operating; its library holds manuscripts going back over a thousand years."],
"Igbo-Ukwu bronzes":["At Igbo-Ukwu in southeastern Nigeria, around 900, artists cast bronze bowls, shells and ornaments covered in delicate patterns of insects and beads, using the lost-wax method.","A farmer digging a well found them in 1938. They show a wealthy Igbo society with long-distance trade, with glass beads from as far as India, centuries before Benin's bronzes."],
"Fatimid Caliphate":["In 909 the Fatimids, Ismaili Shia leaders claiming descent from the Prophet's daughter Fatima, took power in Tunisia with the help of Kutama Berbers, and declared themselves caliphs.","They challenged the Abbasids of Baghdad, conquered Egypt in 969, and at their height ruled from Sicily to the Hejaz."],
"Cairo founded":["In 969 the Fatimid general Jawhar founded a walled palace city beside Fustat and named it al-Qahira, 'the victorious'.","The Fatimids built the al-Azhar mosque in 970, which grew into one of the oldest and most important centres of Islamic learning. Cairo became the largest city in Africa."],
"Oduduwa founds Ile-Ife":["Yoruba tradition tells that Olodumare, the supreme god, sent Oduduwa down from heaven on a chain with sand and a cockerel. He poured the sand onto the waters, the cockerel scattered it, and land was formed at Ile-Ife.","His children founded the other Yoruba kingdoms. Ife's artists later made naturalistic bronze and terracotta heads of astonishing skill. The Ooni of Ife remains the spiritual head of the Yoruba."],
"Mapungubwe":["Mapungubwe, on the Limpopo river where South Africa, Botswana and Zimbabwe meet, was a kingdom from about 1075 to 1290. Its rulers lived on a sandstone hilltop above the commoners, a new kind of social divide.","They traded gold and ivory for glass beads and cloth from the Indian Ocean. A small gold-foil rhinoceros found in a royal grave has become a national treasure of South Africa."],
"Almoravids & Ghana":["The Almoravids were a Berber movement of strict Islam that rose in the western Sahara and conquered Morocco and Muslim Spain, founding Marrakesh in 1070.","Later Arab writers say they conquered Ghana around 1076. Historians now debate whether there was a conquest at all; Ghana did decline in this period, as trade shifted east."],
"Swahili Coast":["From around 1100, city-states such as Kilwa, Mombasa, Lamu and Zanzibar grew rich trading gold, ivory and enslaved people from the interior for Indian cloth, Persian pottery and Chinese porcelain.","Their language, Swahili, is a Bantu language with many Arabic words. The Kilwa Chronicle tells how the city was founded by a Persian prince from Shiraz; archaeology shows the towns were African in origin."],
"Zagwe dynasty":["The Zagwe kings, from the Agaw people of the Ethiopian highlands, ruled from about 1137 to 1270.","Later chroniclers of the Solomonic dynasty called them usurpers, but the Ethiopian church honours several Zagwe kings as saints, including Lalibela."],
"Maimonides in Cairo":["Moses ben Maimon, born in Córdoba, fled persecution by the Almohads and settled in Fustat, beside Cairo, around 1166. He became physician to Saladin's vizier and court and head of Egypt's Jewish community.","He wrote the Mishneh Torah, a code of Jewish law, and the Guide for the Perplexed, in Arabic, reconciling faith with Aristotle's philosophy. Letters in his own hand survive in the Cairo Geniza."],
"Lalibela churches":["King Lalibela had eleven churches carved downward into the volcanic rock at Roha, with trenches and tunnels linking them. Bete Giyorgis, cut in the shape of a cross, is the most famous.","Tradition says he wanted a new Jerusalem, and that angels worked by night. The churches remain in use, and thousands of pilgrims gather there for Christmas."],
"Solomonic dynasty":["In 1270 Yekuno Amlak overthrew the last Zagwe king and founded the Solomonic dynasty, claiming descent from Solomon and the Queen of Sheba through Menelik I.","The Kebra Nagast gave the claim its story. The dynasty ruled, with interruptions, until Haile Selassie was deposed in 1974."],
"Ibn Battuta in Mali":["In 1352 the Moroccan traveller Ibn Battuta crossed the Sahara and spent eight months in Mali under Mansa Suleyman.","He praised the land's safety, saying a traveller had nothing to fear from robbers, and the people's devotion to prayer. He complained about the food, the mansa's stinginess and the freedom of women."],
"Kingdom of Kongo":["Around 1390, Lukeni lua Nimi, by Kongo tradition, crossed the Congo river and founded the kingdom of Kongo, with its capital at Mbanza Kongo.","The kingdom grew to rule millions in today's Angola and the two Congos, with provinces, taxes and a currency of nzimbu shells."]
});
