window.STORY6=Object.assign(window.STORY6||{},{
"Shango, the thunder king":["Oyo tradition remembers Shango as the fourth Alaafin, a fiery king who could breathe fire and call down lightning. After a disaster, in some tellings when his lightning burned his own palace, he went into the forest and hanged himself; his followers declared 'Oba ko so', 'the king did not hang', and he became the orisha of thunder.","Enslaved Yoruba people carried Shango to the Americas, where he is honoured in Cuban Santería as Changó and in Brazilian Candomblé as Xangô."],
"Zara Yaqob":["Zara Yaqob, emperor of Ethiopia from 1434 to 1468, was a fierce church reformer. He wrote religious works himself, enforced observance of the Sabbath, and punished those who worshipped other gods.","He sent envoys to the Council of Florence in 1441 and built a new capital at Debre Berhan, after a light from heaven he saw there."],
"Mutapa":["According to Shona tradition, around 1430 Nyatsimba Mutota led his people north from Great Zimbabwe in search of salt and conquered the Zambezi valley. His people called him Mwene Mutapa, 'lord of the conquered lands'.","Mutapa controlled the gold trade with Swahili and later Portuguese merchants. The Portuguese tried to dominate it, and a later ruler, Mavura, was forced to accept them as overlords in 1629."],
"Songhai Empire":["Sunni Ali, a king of Gao, took Timbuktu in 1468 and Djenné after a long siege. Oral tradition remembers him as a sorcerer-king; Muslim scholars of Timbuktu, whom he persecuted, called him a tyrant.","His successor Askia Muhammad, a general who seized power in 1493, made the pilgrimage to Mecca and built a vast state of provinces and governors. Songhai was the largest empire in West African history."],
"Dias rounds the Cape":["In 1488 Bartolomeu Dias, blown south by a storm, rounded the southern tip of Africa without seeing it, then landed at Mossel Bay. Khoikhoi herders there threw stones at the strangers, and one was killed by a crossbow.","Dias named the point the Cape of Storms; King John II renamed it the Cape of Good Hope. The sea route to India lay open."],
"Gondar":["Around 1636 Emperor Fasilides founded Gondar as Ethiopia's permanent capital, ending centuries of moving royal camps.","He and his successors built castles, churches, libraries and baths. The ceiling of Debre Birhan Selassie church is painted with rows of angels' faces."],
"Dutch at the Cape":["In 1652 Jan van Riebeeck founded a station at the Cape for the Dutch East India Company to supply passing ships. The Khoikhoi had grazed cattle there for centuries.","Conflict over land soon broke out. The colonists imported enslaved people from Asia and Africa. Krotoa, a Khoikhoi woman, worked as interpreter, married a Danish surgeon, and became a symbol of the colony's tangled beginnings."],
"Asante Empire":["Around 1670 Osei Tutu, with the priest Okomfo Anokye, united the Akan states around Kumasi, defeating their overlord Denkyira in 1701.","Asante grew rich in gold, kola and the slave trade, and fought Britain repeatedly in the 1800s. The Asantehene still reigns in Kumasi."],
"Moulay Ismail":["Moulay Ismail ruled Morocco from 1672 to 1727, one of the longest reigns in its history. He built Meknes into an imperial city with vast stables and granaries.","He raised an army of enslaved Black soldiers, the Abid al-Bukhari, and drove the English from Tangier and the Spanish from several ports. European writers called him cruel; Moroccan historians remember him for restoring order."],
"The Golden Stool":["Asante tradition says that around 1701 the priest Okomfo Anokye drew a golden stool down from the sky, which landed on Osei Tutu's knees. It holds the sunsum, the soul, of the Asante nation.","No one sits on it, not even the king. In 1900 the British governor demanded to sit on it, sparking the War of the Golden Stool, led by the queen mother Yaa Asantewaa. The stool was hidden and never surrendered."],
"Bambara Empire":["Around 1712, Mamari Kulibali turned a young men's association into an army and founded the Bambara Empire at Ségou on the Niger.","Bambara rulers followed traditional religion, not Islam. The kingdom lasted until 1861, when it fell to the jihad of El Hadj Umar Tall."],
"Futa Jallon":["Around 1725, Fulani Muslim leaders launched a jihad in the Futa Jallon highlands of Guinea, and created an Islamic state ruled by an almamy.","Two leading families took turns ruling. Futa Jallon became a centre of Islamic learning and of the slave trade. Ayuba Suleiman Diallo, enslaved in Maryland, wrote memoirs about the region."],
"Dahomey's women soldiers":["The kingdom of Dahomey, in today's Benin, had a corps of women soldiers by the early 1700s. The Fon called them Agojie or Mino, 'our mothers'.","Numbering thousands at their height, they fought Dahomey's wars, including slave raids and battles against France in 1890 and 1892. The last known veteran, Nawi, died in 1979."],
"Oyo Empire at its height":["In the 1700s, the Yoruba empire of Oyo, built on cavalry, dominated the savanna west of the Niger and made Dahomey pay tribute.","The Alaafin's power was checked by a council of nobles, the Oyo Mesi, who could require a king to take his own life. Oyo collapsed in the 1830s."],
"Ethiopia's Era of the Princes":["In 1769 the warlord Ras Mikael Sehul had Emperor Iyoas killed. For the next 86 years, regional lords fought for power while emperors were puppets in Gondar.","Ethiopians call it the Zemene Mesafint, the 'Era of the Princes', after a line in the Book of Judges. It ended when Tewodros II was crowned in 1855."],
"Cape Frontier Wars":["From 1779 to 1879, the Xhosa fought nine wars against Dutch and British colonists over land and cattle along the Cape frontier.","In 1856 and 1857, after the prophetess Nongqawuse said the ancestors would return if the Xhosa killed their cattle, perhaps 40,000 people starved."],
"Freetown":["In 1787 British abolitionists sent about 400 Black Londoners, many of them formerly enslaved, to Sierra Leone. Most died or scattered.","In 1792 over 1,000 Black Loyalists from Nova Scotia, who had fought for Britain in the American Revolution, founded Freetown. Their descendants, the Krio, shaped the city."],
"Napoleon in Egypt":["In 1798 Napoleon invaded Egypt with an army and 167 scholars. He defeated the Mamluks at the Battle of the Pyramids, but Nelson sank his fleet.","The Egyptian historian al-Jabarti described the French with curiosity and contempt. The scholars' Description of Egypt began modern Egyptology."],
"Sokoto Caliphate":["In 1804 the Fulani scholar Usman dan Fodio declared a jihad against the Hausa kings, accusing them of corruption.","He founded the Sokoto Caliphate, one of the largest states in 1800s Africa. His daughter Nana Asma'u wrote poetry and set up a network of women teachers."],
"Muhammad Ali of Egypt":["Muhammad Ali, an Albanian officer sent by the Ottomans, took power in Egypt in 1805. In 1811 he massacred the Mamluk leaders at a banquet.","He built a conscript army, factories and schools, sent students to Europe, and made cotton Egypt's great export. His descendants ruled until 1952."],
"Hieroglyphs deciphered":["In 1822 Jean-François Champollion, studying the Rosetta Stone and other inscriptions, realized hieroglyphs mixed sounds and symbols. Knowing Coptic, the late form of Egyptian, helped.","He reportedly ran to his brother crying 'I've got it!' and fainted."],
"Liberia independent":["From 1822 the American Colonization Society, whose members included both abolitionists and slaveholders wanting free Black people out of the US, settled freed African Americans at Cape Mesurado, later Monrovia.","Liberia declared independence on 26 July 1847, with Joseph Jenkins Roberts as president. The settlers, called Americo-Liberians, dominated the Indigenous majority, who could not vote for decades; a coup in 1980 ended their rule."],
"Diamonds at Kimberley":["In 1867 a boy named Erasmus Jacobs found a shiny stone near the Orange River; it was a 21-carat diamond. A rush followed, and miners dug the Big Hole at Kimberley by hand, one of the largest hand-dug holes on Earth.","Cecil Rhodes's De Beers company bought up the claims. Black miners were confined in closed compounds and searched, a system that shaped later migrant labour and apartheid."],
"Suez Canal opens":["The Suez Canal was dug between 1859 and 1869 by a French company under Ferdinand de Lesseps. Tens of thousands of Egyptian peasants were forced to work on it under the corvée, and many died.","It opened in November 1869 with a lavish celebration. Egypt's debts forced Khedive Ismail to sell his shares to Britain in 1875, and Britain occupied Egypt in 1882."],
"Anglo-Zulu War":["In January 1879 Britain invaded the Zulu kingdom after King Cetshwayo refused an ultimatum to disband his army. On 22 January, at Isandlwana, some 20,000 Zulu warriors overran a British camp, killing over 1,300 soldiers.","Zulu veterans later told how they advanced in the 'horns of the buffalo' formation. Britain sent reinforcements, burned the royal capital at Ulundi in July, and broke up the kingdom."],
"Congo Free State":["At the Berlin Conference in 1885, King Leopold II of Belgium won recognition of the Congo as his personal property. His agents forced villagers to collect wild rubber, holding wives hostage and cutting off hands as punishment.","Millions died from violence, famine and disease. The African American missionary William Sheppard, the clerk Edmund Morel and the diplomat Roger Casement exposed the abuses, and photographs by Alice Seeley Harris showed the world. Belgium took over the colony in 1908."],
"Boer War":["From 1899 to 1902 Britain fought the two Boer republics, the Transvaal and the Orange Free State, which held the world's richest gold mines.","To defeat Boer guerrillas, Britain burned farms and held Boer women and children in concentration camps, where about 26,000 died; at least 20,000 Black Africans died in separate camps. Emily Hobhouse exposed the conditions."],
"Herero & Nama genocide":["In January 1904 the Herero rose against German settlers who had taken their land and cattle in Namibia. After defeating them at Waterberg, General Lothar von Trotha drove them into the Omaheke desert and ordered that every Herero be shot.","About 80 percent of the Herero and half of the Nama died, in the desert and in concentration camps such as Shark Island. Germany acknowledged it as genocide in 2021 and offered development aid, which many Herero and Nama leaders rejected."],
"Maji Maji Rebellion":["In 1905 peoples of southern German East Africa, forced to grow cotton for export, rose against German rule. The prophet Kinjikitile Ngwale gave fighters maji, holy water, said to turn bullets into water.","The rebellion united over twenty peoples. Germany's response, burning crops and villages, caused a famine that killed perhaps 200,000 to 300,000 people. Tanzanians remember it as a forerunner of the independence struggle."],
"Union of South Africa":["On 31 May 1910, the British colonies of the Cape and Natal joined the former Boer republics to form the Union of South Africa, a self-governing dominion.","Black and Coloured people were shut out of power almost everywhere. The 1913 Natives Land Act barred Black South Africans from owning land in most of the country; Sol Plaatje wrote that they woke up 'pariahs in the land of their birth'."],
"ANC founded":["On 8 January 1912, African chiefs, lawyers, teachers and clergy met in Bloemfontein and founded the South African Native National Congress, renamed the African National Congress in 1923.","For decades it petitioned peacefully. After the 1960 Sharpeville massacre it was banned and turned to armed struggle. It won the first democratic election in 1994."],
"Egypt independent":["After the 1919 revolution, when Egyptians of all classes and faiths protested British rule, Britain declared Egypt independent on 28 February 1922, but kept control of defence, the Suez Canal and Sudan.","King Fuad I took the throne. Months later, Howard Carter found the tomb of Tutankhamun, and Egyptians insisted the treasures stay in Egypt."],
"Italy invades Ethiopia":["In October 1935 Mussolini invaded Ethiopia, avenging Italy's defeat at Adwa. Italian forces dropped mustard gas on soldiers and civilians.","Emperor Haile Selassie warned the League of Nations: 'It is us today. It will be you tomorrow.' Ethiopian resistance fighters, the Arbegnoch, fought on, and the emperor returned in 1941 with British help."],
"Apartheid":["In 1948 the National Party won a whites-only election and began apartheid, 'separateness'. Laws classified every person by race, banned mixed marriages, and forced people into separate areas.","Millions were forcibly removed from their homes, as in Sophiatown and District Six. Black South Africans were assigned to 'homelands' and had to carry passbooks."],
"Mau Mau uprising":["In 1952 Kikuyu fighters, many of them landless, launched a revolt against British rule and settler land seizures in Kenya. They called themselves the Kenya Land and Freedom Army.","Britain declared an emergency, held tens of thousands in detention camps where torture was common, and hanged over 1,000 people. In 2013 Britain paid compensation to over 5,000 survivors."],
"Suez Crisis":["In July 1956 Egypt's president Gamal Abdel Nasser nationalized the Suez Canal. In October and November, Israel, Britain and France invaded, by secret agreement.","The United States and the Soviet Union forced them to withdraw. The crisis made Nasser a hero across the Arab world and marked the end of Britain as a great power."],
"Sharpeville massacre":["On 21 March 1960, thousands gathered at the police station in Sharpeville to protest the pass laws. Police opened fire, killing 69 people, many shot in the back.","The ANC and PAC were banned, and turned to armed struggle. 21 March is now Human Rights Day in South Africa."],
"Lumumba killed":["Patrice Lumumba became the Congo's first prime minister at independence in June 1960. In his independence day speech he denounced the humiliations of colonial rule in front of the Belgian king.","Within months, amid secession and army mutiny, he was overthrown. On 17 January 1961 he was executed in Katanga with Belgian involvement; the CIA had also plotted his death. In 2022 Belgium returned a tooth, his only remains, to his family."],
"Organisation of African Unity":["On 25 May 1963, 32 newly independent African states founded the Organisation of African Unity in Addis Ababa. 25 May is celebrated as Africa Day.","It supported liberation movements against colonial and white-minority rule, but upheld colonial borders and avoided criticizing members. It became the African Union in 2002."],
"Mandela imprisoned":["In the Rivonia Trial, Nelson Mandela and his co-accused faced the death penalty for sabotage. On 20 April 1964 Mandela told the court that a free society was an ideal for which he was prepared to die.","They were sentenced to life. Mandela spent 18 years on Robben Island breaking rocks in a lime quarry, and 27 years in prison in all."],
"Nigerian Civil War":["In May 1967, after massacres of Igbo people in northern Nigeria, the Eastern Region under Odumegwu Ojukwu declared independence as Biafra.","Nigeria's blockade caused famine; images of starving children shocked the world. Perhaps one to two million people died before Biafra surrendered in January 1970. Chinua Achebe and Chimamanda Ngozi Adichie wrote about it."],
"Idi Amin":["In January 1971 army commander Idi Amin seized power in Uganda. In 1972 he expelled the country's Asian minority, about 80,000 people, giving them 90 days to leave.","His regime killed perhaps 100,000 to 500,000 people. He was driven out in 1979 by Tanzanian troops and Ugandan exiles."],
"Soweto uprising":["On 16 June 1976, thousands of Soweto schoolchildren marched against being taught in Afrikaans. Police opened fire.","Twelve-year-old Hector Pieterson was among the first killed; a photo of him being carried became an icon. Hundreds died in the protests that followed. 16 June is now Youth Day."],
"Ethiopian famine":["In 1983 to 1985, drought, war and government policies brought famine to northern Ethiopia. Perhaps hundreds of thousands to a million people died.","BBC reports by Michael Buerk and Mohamed Amin moved the world, leading to Band Aid and Live Aid. The Derg government also forcibly resettled hundreds of thousands."],
"Second Congo War":["From 1998 to 2003, armies from nine African countries and many militias fought in the Democratic Republic of the Congo over power and minerals.","Millions died, mostly from disease and hunger. Fighting continues in the east."],
"Tunisian revolution":["On 17 December 2010, Mohamed Bouazizi, a street vendor in Sidi Bouzid, set himself on fire after police seized his cart. Protests spread.","On 14 January 2011, President Ben Ali fled after 23 years in power. Uprisings spread across the Arab world."],
"Sudan's civil war begins":["On 15 April 2023, fighting broke out between Sudan's army and the Rapid Support Forces, a paramilitary group that grew out of the Janjaweed militias of Darfur.","By 2026, nearly 11 million people had been displaced, the world's largest displacement crisis, and famine was declared in parts of the country."],
"Morocco earthquake & Libya floods":["On 8 September 2023, an earthquake struck Morocco's High Atlas, killing about 3,000 people in mountain villages.","Days later, Storm Daniel burst two dams above Derna in Libya; a wall of water swept away much of the city, killing thousands."],
"El Fasher falls":["On 26 October 2025, after an 18-month siege, the RSF captured El Fasher, the last army-held city in Darfur.","Survivors described mass killings as people fled. A UN inquiry later found 'hallmarks of genocide'."],
"Morocco's first woman prime minister":["On 29 September 2026, Fatima Ezzahra El Mansouri was appointed Morocco's prime minister after her party won the September election.","She is the first woman to hold the post."]
});
