window.STORY6=Object.assign(window.STORY6||{},{
"Walls of Jericho":["Around 8000 BCE the people of Jericho, near a spring in the Jordan valley, built a stone wall and an 8.5-metre stone tower with an inner staircase. It is one of the oldest monumental structures known.","Kathleen Kenyon excavated it in the 1950s. Jericho's people also buried their dead under their floors and plastered the skulls of some ancestors, setting shells in the eye sockets."],
"Çatalhöyük":["Çatalhöyük, on the Konya plain in Turkey, held perhaps 5,000 to 8,000 people around 7400 to 6000 BCE. Houses were packed so tightly that there were no streets; people walked across the rooftops and climbed down ladders.","Inside, they painted walls with hunting scenes, set bull skulls into the walls, and buried their dead under the floors. Graves suggest men and women lived similar lives."],
"Irrigation canals":["Around 5500 BCE, farmers of the Samarra culture in central Mesopotamia dug canals to carry river water to their fields, at sites such as Choga Mami.","Irrigation let farming spread into the dry south, where the rich soil could feed many. It also required organizing labour, which helped build the first cities."],
"Ubaid culture & Eridu":["In the Ubaid period, from about 6500 to 3800 BCE, farmers in southern Mesopotamia built villages of mud brick. At Eridu, archaeologists found a sequence of temples rebuilt on the same spot for over 2,000 years.","The Sumerian King List says kingship first came down from heaven at Eridu. Its god was Enki, lord of fresh water and wisdom."],
"Creation of the world (Ussher)":["In 1650 James Ussher, Archbishop of Armagh in Ireland, published a chronology that added up the ages in biblical genealogies and matched them with ancient history. He dated creation to the evening before 23 October 4004 BCE.","His dates were printed in the margins of many English Bibles for two centuries. Geology and physics now show the Earth is about 4.5 billion years old."],
"Uruk, the first true city":["By around 4000 to 3100 BCE, Uruk in southern Iraq had grown into a city of perhaps 40,000 people, with huge temple complexes for the goddess Inanna and the sky god An.","Here scribes invented writing, artisans mass-produced bowls, and administrators managed the city's wealth. In legend it was the city of the hero Gilgamesh."],
"Hebrew calendar epoch":["The Jewish calendar counts years from creation, Anno Mundi, using a date calculated in rabbinic tradition from the Bible's chronology: 3761 BCE.","The system was fixed by the 10th century. The Hebrew year 5787 began in September 2026, at Rosh Hashanah."],
"The wheel":["Wheeled vehicles appear around 3500 BCE in Mesopotamia, the Caucasus and Europe within a few centuries. The oldest surviving wheel, from the Ljubljana marshes in Slovenia, is about 5,150 years old.","Early wheels were solid wood, used on carts pulled by oxen. Potters in Mesopotamia may have used turning wheels even earlier."],
"Bronze Age begins":["Around 3300 BCE, smiths in the Near East learned to mix copper with tin, or arsenic, to make bronze, which is harder and casts better than copper.","Tin was rare, found in places like Afghanistan, Anatolia and Cornwall, so bronze drove long-distance trade."],
"Death of Adam (Ussher)":["Genesis 5:5 says Adam lived 930 years. On Ussher's chronology, that puts his death in 3074 BCE.","This is close to the time when writing was invented at Uruk, a coincidence some writers find striking. The date is a calculation from scripture, not a historical event."],
"Counting in sixties":["Sumerians counted in base 60, perhaps because 60 can be divided evenly by 2, 3, 4, 5, 6, 10, 12, 15, 20 and 30.","Babylonian astronomers inherited the system, and Greeks borrowed it. That is why we still have 60 seconds, 60 minutes and 360 degrees."],
"Early Dynastic Sumer":["From about 2900 BCE, Sumer was a land of rival city-states, among them Ur, Uruk, Lagash, Umma and Kish, each with its own god and ruler.","They fought over land and water. The Sumerian King List tries to arrange their rulers in a single line, some with reigns of thousands of years."],
"Etana's flight":["The Sumerian King List names Etana of Kish as 'a shepherd, who ascended to heaven'. In a later Babylonian epic, he is childless and rescues an eagle trapped in a pit.","The eagle carries him up to the heavens to find the plant of birth. The ending is lost, but the King List says his son succeeded him."],
"Royal Tombs of Ur":["In the 1920s, Leonard Woolley, with Iraqi workers, excavated about 1,800 graves at Ur. Sixteen were lavish royal tombs of about 2600 BCE.","Queen Puabi was buried with a headdress of gold leaves, lapis lazuli and carnelian. Dozens of attendants lay with her, apparently sacrificed. The tombs also held the Standard of Ur and lyres with bull heads."],
"Ebla":["In 1974 and 1975, Italian archaeologists at Tell Mardikh in Syria found the archives of Ebla, about 17,000 tablets and fragments from around 2350 BCE.","They are written in Sumerian and Eblaite, a Semitic language, and record trade, treaties and the oldest known bilingual dictionaries. Ebla was destroyed, perhaps by Akkad."],
"Lagash vs. Umma":["Around 2450 BCE, Eannatum of Lagash defeated the neighbouring city of Umma in a war over a fertile border region.","His Stele of the Vultures shows the god Ningirsu catching enemies in a net, and soldiers marching in a phalanx over bodies as vultures carry off heads. It is the oldest known war monument."],
"Sargon of Akkad":["Around 2334 BCE, Sargon, a cupbearer to the king of Kish, seized power and conquered all of Sumer, then lands to the Mediterranean and Iran, creating the first empire.","A later legend, written in his voice, says his mother, a priestess, set him adrift in a reed basket sealed with bitumen, and he was found and raised by a gardener. His dynasty lasted about 180 years."],
"Enheduanna":["Enheduanna, Sargon's daughter, was high priestess of the moon god Nanna at Ur. She wrote hymns to the goddess Inanna, including the Exaltation of Inanna.","In one hymn she tells of being driven from her temple by a rebel and calling on Inanna to restore her. She is the earliest author known by name."],
"Tower of Babel":["Genesis tells how people built a city and a tower reaching to heaven. God confused their language and scattered them across the earth.","The story is likely inspired by Babylon's great ziggurat, Etemenanki, 'House of the foundation of heaven and earth', which Jewish exiles would have seen."],
"Ziggurat & laws of Ur":["Ur-Nammu founded the Third Dynasty of Ur around 2112 BCE and built the Great Ziggurat for the moon god Nanna. Its lower levels still stand.","His law code is the oldest known. Unlike later codes, many penalties were fines rather than physical punishments: for example, a set amount of silver for cutting off a man's foot."]
});
