window.STORY6=Object.assign(window.STORY6||{},{
"Fall of Ur":["In 2004 BCE, the Elamites from Iran sacked Ur and carried off its last king, Ibbi-Sin, ending the Third Dynasty of Ur.","Sumerian poets wrote the Lament for Ur, in which the goddess Ningal weeps for her ruined city, its dead lying in the streets 'like fat placed in the sun'."],
"Assyrian merchants at Kanesh":["Around 1950 to 1750 BCE, merchants from the city of Ashur in Assyria ran a trading colony at Kanesh, near Kayseri in Turkey. They sent donkey caravans carrying tin and textiles across 1,000 km.","Over 23,000 clay letters survive, many written by the merchants' wives in Ashur, who managed businesses and produced the textiles. One woman scolded her husband: 'You love money and hate your life'."],
"Abraham":["In Genesis, God calls Abraham to leave Ur and go to Canaan, promising him countless descendants. Jews, Christians and Muslims honour him; in Islamic tradition, Ibrahim and his son Ismail build the Kaaba in Mecca.","Tradition places him around 2000 to 1800 BCE. There is no archaeological evidence of him as a person, and many scholars see the stories as written down much later."],
"Inanna's Descent":["In this Sumerian poem, the goddess Inanna goes down to the underworld, ruled by her sister Ereshkigal. At each of seven gates she must give up a garment or jewel, until she stands naked and is killed and hung on a hook.","The god Enki sends helpers to revive her, but she must send someone in her place. She chooses her husband Dumuzi, who did not mourn her. It is one of the oldest myths of death and return."],
"Plimpton 322":["This Babylonian clay tablet, written around 1800 BCE, lists 15 sets of numbers that fit the rule a² + b² = c², what we call Pythagorean triples.","It predates Pythagoras by over a thousand years. Scholars debate its purpose, perhaps a teacher's aid, perhaps a trigonometric table."],
"Glass making":["Around 1600 BCE, craftsmen in Mesopotamia began making glass vessels, by winding molten glass around a core.","Glass was a luxury, coloured to imitate precious stones. A Mesopotamian text gives recipes for glass in a secret code."],
"Hittites sack Babylon":["Around 1595 BCE, the Hittite king Mursili I marched over 2,000 km to Babylon, sacked it and carried off the statue of its god Marduk.","He returned home and was assassinated. The Kassites, from the Zagros, took over Babylon and ruled for over 400 years."],
"Battle of Megiddo":["Around 1457 BCE, Thutmose III of Egypt surprised a Canaanite coalition at Megiddo by leading his army through a narrow pass. His scribe Tjaneni recorded it, carved on the temple walls at Karnak.","His troops stopped to plunder the enemy camp, and the city held out for seven months. Megiddo, in Hebrew Har Megiddo, gave the word Armageddon."],
"Joshua at Jericho":["The Book of Joshua tells how the Israelites marched around Jericho for seven days, blew trumpets and shouted, and the walls fell down flat.","Excavations by Kathleen Kenyon found that Jericho was largely abandoned or barely walled at the time; many scholars see the story as a later tradition."],
"Amarna letters":["In 1887, a woman digging at Amarna in Egypt found clay tablets. They are letters, in Babylonian cuneiform, from about 1360 to 1330 BCE, between pharaohs and the kings of Babylon, Mitanni, Assyria and the Hittites, and Canaanite vassals.","Kings call each other 'brother' and haggle over gold and marriages. Canaanite rulers beg for help against the 'Habiru', bands of outcasts."],
"Kumarbi & the storm god":["In Hittite myths based on Hurrian stories, the god Kumarbi overthrows the sky god Anu by biting off his genitals. Swallowing them, he becomes pregnant with the storm god Teshub, who later overthrows him.","Kumarbi creates a stone monster, Ullikummi, to fight back. The pattern of gods overthrowing their fathers is very like Hesiod's Greek myth of Kronos and Zeus."],
"Baal Cycle":["Tablets from Ugarit tell how the storm god Baal fought Yam, the Sea, with two magic clubs, and won a palace from the high god El.","He then entered the realm of Mot, Death, and was swallowed, until his sister Anat defeated Mot. Baal's return brought the rains. The Hebrew Bible condemns the worship of Baal."],
"Chariot warfare peaks":["By the 1300s BCE, chariots dominated warfare across the Near East. The Hittites used heavy chariots with three men, the Egyptians light chariots with two.","At Kadesh in 1274 BCE, perhaps 5,000 chariots fought, the largest chariot battle known. Chariot armies collapsed with the Bronze Age world."],
"Battle of Kadesh":["In 1274 BCE, Ramesses II marched into Syria to fight the Hittite king Muwatalli II. Misled by spies, he was ambushed near Kadesh but fought his way out.","Ramesses carved his version of a great victory on temple walls across Egypt. Hittite records suggest a stalemate. Some years later, the two empires made the oldest surviving peace treaty."],
"The Exodus":["The Book of Exodus tells how Moses led the Israelites out of slavery in Egypt, through the parted Red Sea, to Mount Sinai, where God gave the Ten Commandments.","Jews remember it every year at Passover. No Egyptian record or archaeological evidence confirms it; scholars who look for a setting usually point to the reign of Ramesses II."],
"Enuma Elish":["The Babylonian creation epic, named after its opening words, 'When on high', tells how the young god Marduk defeated the sea dragon Tiamat.","He split her body in two to make the sky and earth, and made humans from the blood of her general Kingu to serve the gods. It was recited at Babylon's New Year festival."],
"Samson & Delilah":["In the Book of Judges, Samson, a Nazirite, kills a lion with his bare hands and a thousand Philistines with a donkey's jawbone. His strength lies in his uncut hair.","Delilah betrays him to the Philistines, who blind him. In the temple of Dagon, he pulls down the pillars, killing himself and his enemies."],
"David & Goliath":["In the Book of Samuel, the shepherd boy David, refusing armour, kills the Philistine giant Goliath with a stone from his sling.","David became king of Israel. Another verse credits the killing of Goliath to Elhanan, one of David's warriors, which scholars think may be the original."],
"Zoroaster":["Zoroaster, in Avestan Zarathustra, was an Iranian prophet who taught that the world is a struggle between Ahura Mazda, the wise lord, and the destructive spirit Angra Mainyu.","His own hymns, the Gathas, survive. His dates are deeply uncertain, anywhere from 1500 to 600 BCE. Zoroastrianism shaped ideas of heaven, hell and a final judgment."],
"Yima / Jamshid":["In Iranian tradition, Yima, called Jamshid in Persian, was the first king, ruling over a golden age when there was no death or disease.","When he grew proud, his glory left him. The Shahnameh says Nowruz, the Persian New Year, began when Jamshid rose into the sky on a jewelled throne."]
});
