window.STORY6=Object.assign(window.STORY6||{},{
"Solomon's Temple":["The Book of Kings says Solomon built a temple in Jerusalem with cedar from Lebanon, supplied by Hiram of Tyre, to house the Ark of the Covenant.","No remains of it have been found, partly because the Temple Mount cannot be excavated. It was destroyed by the Babylonians in 587 BCE."],
"Kingdom of Urartu":["Urartu, centred on Lake Van, was a powerful kingdom from about 860 to 590 BCE, a rival of Assyria. Its people called their land Biainili.","They built fortresses, canals and terraces, and were skilled bronze workers. The name Urartu survives in Mount Ararat. Armenians regard Urartu as part of their heritage."],
"Battle of Qarqar":["In 853 BCE the Assyrian king Shalmaneser III fought a coalition of twelve kings at Qarqar in Syria. His inscription lists their forces, including 2,000 chariots from King Ahab of Israel.","It also mentions 1,000 camels from Gindibu the Arab, the first mention of Arabs in history. Shalmaneser claimed victory, but he had to fight again."],
"Tel Dan Stele":["In 1993 archaeologists at Tel Dan in northern Israel found pieces of an Aramaic inscription from about 840 BCE, in which an Aramean king boasts of killing the kings of Israel and the 'House of David'.","It is the earliest reference outside the Bible to a dynasty founded by David."],
"Jonah & the great fish":["In the Book of Jonah, the prophet flees God's command to preach to Nineveh. Thrown overboard in a storm, he is swallowed by a great fish and spends three days inside it.","He finally preaches, and Nineveh repents and is spared, to his annoyance. In Islam he is the prophet Yunus. His tomb at Mosul was destroyed by ISIS in 2014."],
"Tiglath-Pileser III":["Tiglath-Pileser III seized the Assyrian throne in 745 BCE and built a professional standing army and a network of provinces.","He deported whole populations to break resistance, moving perhaps hundreds of thousands of people. He also made himself king of Babylon."],
"Fall of Israel":["In 722 BCE, after a three-year siege, Assyria took Samaria, capital of the northern kingdom of Israel. Sargon II claimed to have deported 27,290 people.","The deported Israelites vanished from history, giving rise to legends of the Ten Lost Tribes. Samaritans claim descent from Israelites who stayed."],
"Qanats":["Persian engineers dug qanats: gently sloping underground tunnels that carry groundwater from hillsides to fields and towns, sometimes over many kilometres, with vertical shafts for access.","The technique spread across the Middle East, North Africa and Spain. Some qanats in Iran are still in use after 2,000 years."],
"Library of Ashurbanipal":["Ashurbanipal, king of Assyria from 668 BCE, collected tablets from across Mesopotamia at Nineveh. He boasted he could read cuneiform and solve difficult problems.","When Nineveh burned in 612 BCE, the clay tablets baked hard. Found in the 1850s, over 30,000 fragments include the Epic of Gilgamesh, recognized by George Smith in 1872."],
"Babylonian astronomical diaries":["From 652 BCE until the 1st century BCE, Babylonian astronomers kept nightly diaries of the moon, planets and weather, together with river levels, prices and events.","It is the longest scientific record from the ancient world. Modern scientists use it to study the Earth's rotation."],
"Fall of Nineveh":["In 612 BCE, Babylonians and Medes besieged Nineveh, the Assyrian capital, and destroyed it.","The Hebrew prophet Nahum celebrated its fall. Within a few years, the Assyrian Empire was gone."],
"Battle of Carchemish":["In 605 BCE, the Babylonian crown prince Nebuchadnezzar defeated the Egyptian army of Necho II at Carchemish on the Euphrates.","The Babylonian Chronicle records it. Babylon took control of Syria and Judah."],
"First coins":["Around 600 BCE, Lydia, in western Anatolia, began striking coins of electrum, a natural alloy of gold and silver, stamped with a lion's head.","Under King Croesus, Lydia minted separate gold and silver coins. Coinage spread to Greece and Persia."],
"Hanging Gardens of Babylon":["Greek writers describe gardens on terraces, said to have been built by Nebuchadnezzar for his wife from the mountains of Media.","No Babylonian text mentions them, and none have been found. Oxford scholar Stephanie Dalley argues they were at Nineveh, built by Sennacherib."],
"Babylonian exile":["In 587 BCE, Nebuchadnezzar destroyed Jerusalem and its temple and deported much of Judah's elite to Babylon.","Psalm 137 remembers them weeping 'by the rivers of Babylon'. Babylonian records list rations for Jehoiachin, the exiled king of Judah."],
"Croesus falls to Cyrus":["Croesus of Lydia, famed for his wealth, asked the oracle at Delphi whether to attack Persia. He was told that if he did, he would destroy a great empire.","He attacked and was defeated by Cyrus around 547 BCE; the empire he destroyed was his own."],
"Persepolis":["Around 518 BCE, Darius I began building Persepolis, a ceremonial capital on a stone terrace in Fars.","Its reliefs show delegations from across the empire bringing gifts. Tablets found there record payments to workers, including women, with extra rations for new mothers."],
"Judaism takes shape":["During and after the exile in Babylon, Jewish scribes gathered and edited the books of the Hebrew Bible.","Worship focused on one God, the Torah and the Sabbath. Ezra read the law to the people in Jerusalem."],
"Royal Road":["The Persian Royal Road ran about 2,700 km from Sardis to Susa, with relay stations a day's ride apart.","Herodotus said nothing mortal travelled faster than the Persian messengers. His praise is the origin of the motto of the US Postal Service."],
"Esther":["The Book of Esther tells how Esther, a Jewish woman, became queen of Persia and saved her people from a plot by the minister Haman to kill them.","Jews celebrate it at Purim. No Persian source mentions Esther."]
});
