window.STORY6=Object.assign(window.STORY6||{},{
"The Night Journey":["Islamic tradition tells that one night around 621, Muhammad was carried on the winged steed Buraq from Mecca to 'the farthest mosque', understood as Jerusalem, where he led earlier prophets in prayer.","He then rose through the seven heavens to the presence of God, where the five daily prayers were set. The Qur'an alludes to it in Surah al-Isra, and it is why Jerusalem is Islam's third holiest city."],
"Death of Muhammad":["Muhammad died in Medina in June 632, in the house of his wife Aisha. By then most of Arabia had accepted Islam.","His companions chose his friend and father-in-law Abu Bakr as caliph, 'successor'. Some believed Muhammad had chosen his cousin and son-in-law Ali, a disagreement at the root of the Sunni-Shia split."],
"Battle of al-Qadisiyyah":["In 636 or 637, Arab armies under Sa'd ibn Abi Waqqas defeated the Sasanian Persian army at al-Qadisiyyah in Iraq. The Persian general Rostam was killed.","The Arabs took the Persian capital Ctesiphon soon after. Later Persian tradition, in the Shahnameh, mourns the defeat."],
"Zoroastrianism declines":["After the Arab conquest of Iran, the last Sasanian king, Yazdegerd III, was killed in 651. Over centuries most Iranians became Muslim.","Groups of Zoroastrians migrated to Gujarat in India, by tradition around the 8th to 10th century; their descendants are the Parsis. Small communities remain in Iran, in Yazd and Kerman."],
"Umayyad Caliphate":["In 661, after the murder of Ali, Mu'awiya, governor of Syria, became caliph and made Damascus his capital.","Umayyad armies conquered from Spain to Central Asia. Arabic became the language of government and Islamic coinage replaced images with words."],
"Dome of the Rock":["In 691 the Umayyad caliph Abd al-Malik completed the Dome of the Rock in Jerusalem, over the rock where Jewish tradition places the Temple and Muslim tradition Muhammad's ascent.","Its mosaics and inscriptions, the earliest dated Qur'anic text, proclaim the oneness of God. It is among the oldest surviving Islamic buildings."],
"Abbasid Revolution":["In 747, black-bannered rebels rose in Khorasan under Abu Muslim. In 750 the Abbasids, descendants of Muhammad's uncle Abbas, defeated the Umayyads at the Zab River.","They massacred the Umayyad family at a banquet. One prince, Abd al-Rahman, escaped to Spain."],
"One Thousand and One Nights":["In the frame story, King Shahryar marries a new bride each night and executes her at dawn. Scheherazade tells him a story each night, stopping at a cliffhanger, until he spares her.","The tales draw on Persian, Indian and Arabic stories, many set in Harun al-Rashid's Baghdad. Aladdin and Ali Baba were added by the French translator Antoine Galland from a Syrian storyteller, Hanna Diyab."],
"Jabir ibn Hayyan":["Jabir ibn Hayyan, Geber in Latin, is credited with a huge body of works on alchemy, describing distillation, crystallization and acids.","Many of the works were probably written by later followers. Words such as alkali and alembic come from Arabic alchemy."],
"Zanj Rebellion":["Thousands of enslaved East Africans, called Zanj, worked in salt marshes in southern Iraq. In 869 they rose under Ali ibn Muhammad.","They built their own city and held out for 14 years before the Abbasids crushed them."],
"Al-Razi":["Abu Bakr al-Razi, Rhazes, ran a hospital in Baghdad. He chose the site by hanging meat in different places and seeing where it rotted slowest.","His treatise on smallpox and measles was the first to tell them apart."],
"Buyids take Baghdad":["In 945 the Buyids, three brothers from Daylam, a mountainous region by the Caspian, entered Baghdad. They were Shia, but kept the Sunni Abbasid caliph on his throne as a figurehead.", "They ruled Iraq and western Iran for a century, sponsored scholars and public celebrations of Shia festivals such as Ashura, and took the old Persian title shahanshah, 'king of kings'."],
"Shahnameh":["Ferdowsi spent over thirty years writing the Shahnameh, completed in 1010, telling Iran's history from creation to the Arab conquest in about 50,000 couplets.","He wrote in Persian with few Arabic words, helping preserve the language. Its hero Rostam fights the White Div and, tragically, kills his own son Sohrab."],
"Ibn al-Haytham's optics":["Ibn al-Haytham, Alhazen, from Basra, worked in Cairo. His Book of Optics (around 1021) showed that we see because light enters the eye, not because the eye sends out rays.","He insisted on testing ideas by experiment. Legend says he wrote it while feigning madness to escape a caliph's demand."],
"Omar Khayyam's calendar":["In 1079 Omar Khayyam, a mathematician of Nishapur, helped create the Jalali calendar for the Seljuk sultan Malik-Shah. It was more accurate than the later Gregorian calendar.","Khayyam also solved cubic equations geometrically. His quatrains, translated by Edward FitzGerald, became famous in English."],
"The Assassins":["In 1090 Hassan-i Sabbah, an Ismaili leader, seized the mountain fortress of Alamut in northern Iran. His followers carried out targeted killings of enemies.","European crusaders spread legends of hashish-fuelled killers. The Mongols destroyed Alamut in 1256."],
"Ibn al-Nafis":["Ibn al-Nafis, a physician in Cairo and Damascus, described in 1242 how blood passes from the heart's right side through the lungs to the left side.","His work was unknown in Europe until the 20th century."],
"Rumi":["Jalal al-Din Rumi, a Persian poet and Sufi teacher in Konya, wrote the Masnavi, a vast poem of spiritual teaching.","He died in 1273. His followers, the Mevlevi, are known for their whirling dance."],
"Ottomans take Bursa":["In 1326, after a siege lasting years, Osman's son Orhan took the Byzantine city of Bursa, at the foot of Mount Uludağ. Osman, dying, was buried there.", "It became the first Ottoman capital, famed for its silk trade and hot springs. The early sultans' tombs and mosques still stand."],
"Ibn Khaldun's Muqaddimah":["Ibn Khaldun, a Tunisian scholar, wrote the Muqaddimah in 1377, studying why dynasties rise and fall. He argued that group solidarity, asabiyya, drives power.","He is seen as a founder of sociology and economics."]
});
// Full stories: Middle East, part 6.
window.STORY6=Object.assign(window.STORY6||{},{
"Hafez":["Hafez of Shiraz, who died around 1389, wrote ghazals, short lyric poems about love, wine and the divine, mocking religious hypocrites.","Iranians still open his collected poems at random to seek guidance, especially on the winter solstice night of Yalda. His tomb in Shiraz is a place of pilgrimage."],
"Battle of Ankara":["In 1402 Timur defeated and captured the Ottoman sultan Bayezid I near Ankara. Legend says he kept Bayezid in an iron cage.","Bayezid died in captivity. The Ottoman state nearly fell apart in a decade of civil war among his sons, which gave Constantinople half a century more."],
"Coffee":["By the mid-1400s, Sufis in Yemen were brewing coffee from beans brought from Ethiopia to stay awake during night prayers. Ethiopian legend credits a goatherd named Kaldi, whose goats danced after eating the berries.","Coffee spread to Mecca and Cairo, and coffeehouses became places of conversation, which some authorities tried to ban."],
"Safavid Iran":["In 1501 Ismail, the fourteen-year-old leader of the Safavid Sufi order, took Tabriz and declared himself shah. He made Twelver Shia Islam the state religion and imposed it, often by force.","He wrote poetry in Azerbaijani Turkish under the pen name Khatai. The Safavids shaped Iran's lasting Shia identity."],
"Ottomans conquer the Mamluks":["In 1516 and 1517, Selim I defeated the Mamluks at Marj Dabiq and Ridaniya, and took Cairo. The last Mamluk sultan, Tuman Bay, was hanged at a city gate.","The Ottomans gained Syria, Egypt and the guardianship of Mecca and Medina. The Egyptian chronicler Ibn Iyas recorded the city's fall."],
"Suleiman the Magnificent":["Suleiman I ruled from 1520 to 1566. Europeans called him 'the Magnificent'; Ottomans called him Kanuni, 'the Lawgiver', for his legal reforms.","He took Belgrade, Rhodes and Baghdad, won at Mohács and besieged Vienna. His architect Mimar Sinan built the Süleymaniye Mosque. His wife Hürrem, once enslaved, became a powerful figure at court."],
"Coffeehouses in Istanbul":["The first coffeehouses opened in Istanbul around 1555, started by men from Aleppo and Damascus.","People gathered to drink coffee, play backgammon, listen to storytellers and discuss the news. Sultans sometimes closed them as hotbeds of gossip and dissent."],
"Isfahan":["In 1598 Shah Abbas I moved his capital to Isfahan. He built a vast square, Naqsh-e Jahan, surrounded by mosques, a palace and the bazaar.","Persians said 'Isfahan nesf-e jahan': Isfahan is half the world. Abbas also resettled Armenians in a quarter of the city, New Julfa."],
"Ottomans retake Baghdad":["In 1638 Sultan Murad IV retook Baghdad from the Safavids after a siege.","The Treaty of Zuhab in 1639 drew a border between the two empires that roughly matches today's Iran-Iraq border."],
"Tulip Period":["From 1718 to 1730 the Ottoman elite enjoyed gardens, poetry and festivals. Tulips, native to Central Asia, became a craze, with rare bulbs costing fortunes.","The period ended with the Patrona Halil revolt, when angry Istanbul crowds toppled the sultan."],
"Nader Shah":["Nader, a soldier of Turkmen origin, drove out Afghan invaders and seized the Persian throne in 1736.","In 1739 he sacked Delhi and carried off the Peacock Throne and the Koh-i-Noor diamond. His own officers assassinated him in 1747."],
"Wahhabism":["In 1744 the preacher Muhammad ibn Abd al-Wahhab, calling for a strict return to early Islam, made a pact with Muhammad ibn Saud, ruler of Diriyah.","The alliance founded the first Saudi state. Followers rejected shrine visits and saint veneration."],
"Qajar dynasty":["Agha Mohammad Khan, a Qajar Turkmen chief castrated as a boy by a rival, united Iran in the 1780s and 1790s, made Tehran his capital, and was crowned shah in 1796. He was famed for his cruelty, and was murdered by his servants a year later.", "The Qajars ruled until 1925. They lost the Caucasus to Russia in two wars and granted Europeans sweeping concessions, but Qajar painting, photography and court life flourished, and Naser al-Din Shah kept diaries of his travels to Europe."],
"Tanzimat reforms":["In 1839 the Ottoman government read out the Gülhane edict, promising security of life, honour and property, fair taxes and regular conscription. A further edict in 1856 declared Muslims and non-Muslims equal.", "The Tanzimat, 'reorganization', created new courts, schools, a census and a modern bureaucracy. Some Muslims resented losing their privileged place, and many Christians wanted independence instead."],
"Bahá'í Faith begins":["In 1844 in Shiraz, Siyyid Ali-Muhammad declared himself the Báb, 'gate'. He was executed by firing squad in 1850.","His follower Bahá'u'lláh founded the Bahá'í Faith, teaching the unity of humanity. Bahá'ís are still persecuted in Iran."],
"Russo-Turkish War":["In 1877 Russia declared war on the Ottoman Empire, in support of Bulgarian and Serbian uprisings. After a long siege at Pleven, Russian troops reached San Stefano, on the edge of Istanbul.", "The Congress of Berlin in 1878 made Serbia, Montenegro and Romania independent and created a self-governing Bulgaria. Hundreds of thousands of Muslims fled the Balkans and the Caucasus into Anatolia."],
"First Zionist Congress":["In 1897 Theodor Herzl, a Viennese journalist shocked by antisemitism, convened the First Zionist Congress in Basel.","It called for a home for the Jewish people in Palestine. Herzl wrote in his diary that he had founded the Jewish state."],
"Persian Constitutional Revolution":["In 1905 and 1906, Iranian merchants, clerics and reformers protested foreign concessions and royal tyranny. Thousands took sanctuary, bast, in the grounds of the British legation in Tehran.", "Muzaffar al-Din Shah agreed in 1906 to a constitution and a parliament, the Majlis, the first in the Middle East. His successor shelled the Majlis in 1908, but constitutionalists from Tabriz and the Bakhtiari tribes restored it."],
"Young Turk Revolution":["In July 1908, army officers of the Committee of Union and Progress in Macedonia rebelled, forcing Sultan Abdul Hamid II to restore the constitution he had suspended for thirty years. Crowds of Muslims, Christians and Jews celebrated together.", "After a failed counter-coup in 1909 the sultan was deposed. A CUP triumvirate, Enver, Talat and Djemal, took power in 1913, led the empire into World War I, and organized the Armenian genocide."],
"Reza Shah":["Reza Khan, an officer of the Persian Cossack Brigade, took part in a coup in 1921, and in 1925 the Majlis deposed the Qajars and made him shah, founding the Pahlavi dynasty.", "He built the Trans-Iranian Railway, a modern army, schools and Tehran University, and renamed the country Iran in 1935. In 1936 he banned the veil, and police tore headscarves from women. Britain and the Soviet Union forced him to abdicate in 1941."],
"Saudi Arabia founded":["In 1902 Abdulaziz ibn Saud, a young exile in Kuwait, retook his family's former capital Riyadh in a night raid. Over thirty years, with the Ikhwan, tribal warriors of Wahhabi faith, he conquered most of Arabia, including Mecca and Medina in 1924 and 1925.", "In 1932 he proclaimed the Kingdom of Saudi Arabia. He had dozens of sons; his successors as king have all been his sons, and now his grandson's generation is next in line."],
"Oil in Saudi Arabia":["Americans of the Standard Oil Company of California drilled for years near Dammam without success. In March 1938, Dammam No. 7 struck oil in commercial quantities.", "Saudi geologists and workers built the industry that became Saudi Aramco, now fully Saudi-owned. Oil wealth transformed Saudi Arabia and gave the Gulf enormous influence over the world economy."]
});
