window.STORY6=Object.assign(window.STORY6||{},{
"Arab League":["On 22 March 1945, Egypt, Iraq, Jordan (then Transjordan), Lebanon, Saudi Arabia, Syria and Yemen founded the League of Arab States in Cairo, to coordinate policy and protect member states' independence.","It grew to 22 members. It was united in opposing the partition of Palestine, but has often been divided on everything else."],
"State of Israel founded":["On 14 May 1948, as the British Mandate ended, David Ben-Gurion declared the State of Israel in Tel Aviv. Neighbouring Arab states invaded the next day.","Israelis call the war that followed the War of Independence. For Palestinians it is the Nakba, 'catastrophe': about 700,000 fled or were expelled from their homes, and were not allowed to return. Their descendants number millions of refugees today."],
"Coup in Iran":["In 1951 Iran's prime minister Mohammad Mosaddegh nationalized the Anglo-Iranian Oil Company. On 19 August 1953, a coup organized by the CIA and British intelligence, with Iranian royalists, army officers and paid crowds, overthrew him.","The shah returned to rule with growing power. The US acknowledged its role in 2013. Many Iranians date their distrust of the West to this coup."],
"Six-Day War":["On 5 June 1967, after Egypt closed the Straits of Tiran and massed troops in Sinai, Israel launched surprise air strikes. In six days it took Sinai and Gaza from Egypt, the West Bank and East Jerusalem from Jordan, and the Golan Heights from Syria.","Israelis celebrated the return to the Old City and the Western Wall. Arabs call it the Naksa, 'setback'; some 300,000 more Palestinians were displaced. The occupation of the West Bank continues."],
"Yom Kippur War & oil shock":["On 6 October 1973, Yom Kippur, Egypt and Syria attacked Israel to regain the lands lost in 1967. Egyptian troops crossed the Suez Canal; Israel, caught off guard, eventually pushed back.","Arab oil producers cut supplies to countries backing Israel, and oil prices quadrupled. Egyptians remember the crossing as a victory; it led to the peace of 1979."],
"Lebanese Civil War":["From 1975 to 1990, Lebanon's Christian, Sunni, Shia and Druze militias, Palestinian groups, Syria and Israel fought in shifting alliances. Beirut was split by the Green Line.","About 150,000 people died, and thousands disappeared. Massacres included Sabra and Shatila in 1982. The Taif Agreement ended it, sharing power among the religious communities."],
"Camp David Accords":["In September 1978, US president Jimmy Carter hosted Egypt's Anwar Sadat and Israel's Menachem Begin for thirteen days at Camp David. They agreed a framework for peace.","In 1979 Egypt and Israel signed a peace treaty; Israel returned Sinai. The Arab League expelled Egypt, and in 1981 Sadat was assassinated by Islamist army officers."],
"Iranian Revolution":["In 1978 mass protests, strikes and marches spread across Iran against the shah. He left in January 1979, and on 1 February Ayatollah Ruhollah Khomeini returned from exile to cheering crowds.","A referendum declared an Islamic Republic, with Khomeini as Supreme Leader. Many leftists, liberals and women who had joined the revolution were later suppressed. In November, students seized the US embassy and held 52 hostages for 444 days."],
"Iran-Iraq War":["In September 1980 Saddam Hussein invaded Iran. The war lasted eight years, with trench warfare, human-wave attacks and missile strikes on cities.","Iraq used chemical weapons, against Iranian soldiers and against Kurdish civilians at Halabja in 1988, killing about 5,000. Perhaps half a million or more died. The borders were unchanged."],
"First Intifada":["In December 1987, after an Israeli truck killed four Palestinians in Gaza, protests spread across the occupied territories. Young people threw stones; communities organized strikes and boycotts.","The Intifada, 'shaking off', lasted until 1993; over 1,000 Palestinians and about 180 Israelis were killed. Hamas was founded at its start."],
"Gulf War":["In August 1990 Iraq invaded and annexed Kuwait. A US-led coalition of 35 countries launched air strikes in January 1991 and a ground offensive in February that freed Kuwait in 100 hours.","Retreating Iraqis set Kuwait's oil wells on fire. Shia and Kurdish uprisings, encouraged by the US, were crushed by Saddam."],
"Oslo Accords":["After secret talks in Norway, Israel and the PLO recognized each other. On 13 September 1993, Yitzhak Rabin and Yasser Arafat shook hands at the White House.","The Palestinian Authority was created for parts of the West Bank and Gaza. A final settlement was never reached. Rabin was assassinated by an Israeli extremist in 1995."],
"Iraq War":["In March 2003, the US and UK invaded Iraq, claiming Saddam Hussein had weapons of mass destruction; none were found. Baghdad fell in three weeks.","Insurgency and sectarian civil war followed. Hundreds of thousands of Iraqis died. Saddam was hanged in 2006."],
"Burj Khalifa":["The Burj Khalifa opened in Dubai in January 2010. At 828 metres, it is the world's tallest building.","Its design draws on the shape of a desert flower. Thousands of South Asian workers built it, and rights groups criticized their conditions."],
"Arab Spring":["After Tunisia's revolution, protests spread in 2011 to Egypt, where Hosni Mubarak fell after 18 days in Tahrir Square, and to Libya, Yemen, Bahrain and Syria.","Libya, Yemen and Syria fell into civil war. In Syria, the war killed hundreds of thousands and drove over 12 million from their homes."],
"Rise of ISIS":["In June 2014 the Islamic State took Mosul, Iraq's second city, and declared a caliphate across Iraq and Syria. It carried out mass executions and attacks abroad.","It killed and enslaved Yazidis in a genocide in August 2014. Iraqi and Kurdish forces, backed by a coalition, retook Mosul in 2017 and Raqqa that year; the last territory fell in 2019."],
"Hezbollah war & Nasrallah killed":["After 7 October 2023, Hezbollah and Israel exchanged fire across the Lebanese border. In September 2024 thousands of Hezbollah members' pagers and radios exploded.","On 27 September an Israeli air strike in Beirut killed Hezbollah's leader Hassan Nasrallah. A ground invasion followed; a ceasefire took effect in late November."],
"Fall of Assad":["In late November 2024, rebels led by Hay'at Tahrir al-Sham swept from Idlib through Aleppo, Hama and Homs. On 8 December they entered Damascus, and Bashar al-Assad fled to Moscow.","It ended 53 years of Assad family rule. Prisoners were freed from Sednaya prison. HTS leader Ahmed al-Sharaa became interim president in January 2025."],
"Twelve-Day War":["On 13 June 2025, Israel struck Iran's nuclear sites and killed senior commanders and scientists. Iran fired missiles at Israeli cities.","On 22 June US bombers struck the Fordow, Natanz and Isfahan nuclear sites. A ceasefire took effect on 24 June."],
"Gaza ceasefire":["On 10 October 2025, a US-brokered ceasefire took effect in Gaza after two years of war.","The last 20 living Israeli hostages were released on 13 October, and nearly 2,000 Palestinian prisoners. Gazans began returning to ruined homes."],
"Gaza ceasefire: phase two":["In January 2026 a Palestinian technocratic committee was set up to run Gaza under a US-chaired 'Board of Peace'.","Hamas's disarmament and Israel's withdrawal remained disputed."],
"UAE leaves OPEC":["On 28 April 2026, during the war with Iran, the United Arab Emirates announced it would leave OPEC and OPEC+ from 1 May. Abu Dhabi had been a member since 1967.","The UAE had long chafed at production limits, wanting to use its capacity of nearly 5 million barrels a day, and relations with Saudi Arabia had cooled. Its energy minister said the decision was about national interest, not a dispute."]
});
