window.STORY6=Object.assign(window.STORY6||{},{
"Rome at its largest":["Under Trajan, a soldier-emperor born in Spain, Rome reached its greatest extent in 117 CE, from Britain to Mesopotamia, with perhaps 60 million people.","Trajan's Column in Rome spirals with carved scenes of his conquest of Dacia, today's Romania. His successor Hadrian gave up the eastern conquests."],
"Hadrian's Wall":["From 122 CE, the emperor Hadrian had a wall built across northern Britain, 117 km from coast to coast, with forts and milecastles.","Letters written on wooden tablets at the fort of Vindolanda show daily life: a commander's wife inviting a friend to her birthday, soldiers asking for socks and beer. Soldiers came from as far as Syria and North Africa."],
"Marcus Aurelius' Meditations":["Marcus Aurelius, emperor from 161 to 180, spent years on military campaigns on the Danube while plague ravaged the empire.","In his tent he wrote private notes to himself in Greek, now called the Meditations, on Stoic ideas: do your duty, accept what you cannot change, remember that everything passes."],
"Crisis of the Third Century":["From 235 to 284, the Roman Empire had over twenty emperors, most murdered or killed in battle. Plague, inflation and invasions by Goths and Persians struck.","For a time the empire split into three. Diocletian restored order by dividing rule among four emperors."],
"Finn McCool":["In Irish legend, the boy Fionn mac Cumhaill was cooking the Salmon of Knowledge for his teacher, burned his thumb, and sucked it, gaining all the world's wisdom.","He led the Fianna, a band of warrior-poets. In one tale he built the Giant's Causeway to cross to Scotland."],
"Saint George & the dragon":["George was a soldier who, Christians say, was martyred around 303 for refusing to give up his faith, probably at Lydda in Palestine.","The story of him killing a dragon to save a princess appears in the 11th century and spread through the Golden Legend. He is patron saint of England, Georgia, Ethiopia and Catalonia."],
"Council of Nicaea":["In 325 Constantine called bishops to Nicaea to settle a dispute: the priest Arius taught that the Son was created by the Father.","The council declared the Son 'of one substance' with the Father in the Nicene Creed, still recited by many churches. It also set a common way to calculate Easter."],
"Constantinople":["In 330 Constantine dedicated a new capital on the site of Byzantium, on the Bosporus, called Constantinople.","For over a thousand years it was the largest and richest city in Europe, capital of the Eastern Roman Empire."],
"Battle of Adrianople":["In 376 Goths fleeing the Huns were allowed to cross the Danube, but Roman officials mistreated and starved them. They revolted.","In 378 at Adrianople, the Goths destroyed the Roman army and killed Emperor Valens. Ammianus Marcellinus called it the worst Roman defeat since Cannae."],
"Pagan worship banned":["In 391 and 392, the emperor Theodosius I banned pagan sacrifices and closed temples. In Alexandria, Christians destroyed the Serapeum.","The ancient Olympic Games ended soon after. Some pagans blamed the abandonment of the old gods for Rome's troubles; Augustine wrote The City of God in reply."],
"Saint Patrick & the snakes":["Patrick was a Briton kidnapped by Irish raiders at sixteen. In his own Confession, he says he escaped after six years, then returned as a missionary, around 432.","The legend of him driving the snakes from Ireland appears centuries later. Ireland had no snakes after the Ice Age."],
"Clovis baptized":["Clovis, king of the Franks, united most of Gaul. His wife Clotilde was Catholic. Gregory of Tours says that losing a battle, Clovis prayed to her God and won.","He was baptized at Reims around 496, with thousands of warriors. Unlike other Germanic kings, who were Arian, he became Catholic, winning the support of the Gallo-Roman population."],
"King Arthur at Mount Badon":["The British monk Gildas, writing in the 500s, mentions a great British victory over the Saxons at Mount Badon, but not Arthur.","Later Welsh texts credit Arthur. Over centuries, writers added Camelot, the Round Table, Merlin and the Grail. Whether Arthur existed is unknown."],
"Beowulf":["Beowulf, an Old English poem surviving in a single manuscript of about 1000, tells of a Geatish hero who kills the monster Grendel and Grendel's mother in Denmark, and dies fighting a dragon in old age.","The manuscript was damaged in a fire in 1731. J.R.R. Tolkien argued for its importance as poetry."],
"Justinian's Code":["From 529, the emperor Justinian had his jurists, led by Tribonian, collect and organize centuries of Roman law into the Corpus Juris Civilis.","Rediscovered in Italy in the late 1000s, it became the basis of civil law in much of Europe and Latin America."],
"Benedict's monastery":["Around 529 Benedict of Nursia founded a monastery at Monte Cassino and wrote a Rule for monks: prayer, work and reading, under an abbot, in a balanced daily schedule.","His Rule became the model for western monasticism. Monasteries preserved ancient books by copying them."],
"Hagia Sophia":["After riots burned the old church, Justinian had Hagia Sophia, 'Holy Wisdom', built in five years, designed by Anthemius of Tralles and Isidore of Miletus.","Its dome seemed to float on light. Justinian is said to have exclaimed, 'Solomon, I have surpassed you!' It became a mosque in 1453, a museum in 1934, and a mosque again in 2020."],
"Gregory the Great":["Gregory, from a Roman noble family, became pope in 590 and organized relief during plague and Lombard invasions.","Bede tells how he saw fair-haired English slaves in Rome and said they were 'not Angles but angels'. In 597 he sent Augustine to convert the English."],
"Slavs settle the Balkans":["In the 500s and 600s, Slavic peoples crossed the Danube and settled across the Balkans, often with the Avars.","Byzantine writers describe them as living in villages without kings. They became the ancestors of Serbs, Croats, Slovenes, Bulgarians and Macedonians."]
});
