window.STORY6=Object.assign(window.STORY6||{},{
"Mozart's Figaro":["Wolfgang Amadeus Mozart, a child prodigy from Salzburg, premiered The Marriage of Figaro in Vienna in 1786, with a libretto by Lorenzo Da Ponte.","Based on a play banned in France for mocking aristocrats, it shows a servant outwitting his master. Mozart died in 1791 at 35."],
"Lavoisier's chemistry":["In 1789 Antoine Lavoisier published his Elementary Treatise of Chemistry. He showed that burning combines substances with oxygen, that mass is conserved, and gave chemicals systematic names.","His wife Marie-Anne Paulze translated and illustrated his work. He was guillotined in 1794 as a former tax collector."],
"Mary Wollstonecraft":["In 1792 Mary Wollstonecraft published A Vindication of the Rights of Woman, arguing that women were not naturally inferior, only denied education.","She died in 1797 after giving birth to her daughter, Mary Shelley."],
"First vaccine":["In 1796 Edward Jenner, a country doctor, took material from cowpox sores on the milkmaid Sarah Nelmes and put it into a cut on eight-year-old James Phipps. When later exposed to smallpox, James did not fall ill.","'Vaccine' comes from the Latin for cow. Variolation, deliberate infection with mild smallpox, had long been practised in Asia and Africa; Onesimus, an enslaved African in Boston, described it in 1721."],
"Electric battery":["In 1800 Alessandro Volta stacked discs of zinc and copper separated by brine-soaked cloth, producing a steady electric current.","The volt is named after him. Batteries let scientists study electricity."],
"Napoleon crowned":["On 2 December 1804, Napoleon crowned himself emperor in Notre-Dame, taking the crown from the pope's hands. He then crowned Joséphine.","His Civil Code of the same year, with equality before the law and property rights, spread across Europe and Latin America."],
"Grimms' Fairy Tales":["In 1812 the brothers Jacob and Wilhelm Grimm published Children's and Household Tales, collected largely from storytellers, many of them educated young women.","The early editions were darker; later editions softened them for children. Their work fed German national feeling."],
"Waterloo":["On 18 June 1815, Napoleon, returned from exile on Elba, was defeated near Waterloo in Belgium by Wellington's army and Blücher's Prussians.","He was exiled to Saint Helena in the South Atlantic, where he died in 1821."],
"Beethoven's Ninth":["Ludwig van Beethoven was almost totally deaf when his Ninth Symphony premiered in Vienna in 1824. Its last movement sets Schiller's Ode to Joy for chorus.","At the end, a singer turned him round to see the applause he could not hear. The Ode is now the anthem of the European Union."],
"Faraday's induction":["In 1831 Michael Faraday, a blacksmith's son who began as a bookbinder's apprentice, showed that moving a magnet near a wire produces an electric current.","This is the principle of the electric generator. Asked what use it was, he is said to have replied that one day it could be taxed."],
"Kalevala":["In 1835 Elias Lönnrot, a country doctor, published the Kalevala, assembled from folk songs he collected in Karelia.","It tells of the creation of the world from a duck's egg and the magic Sampo. It fed Finland's national movement."],
"Ada Lovelace":["Ada Lovelace, daughter of Lord Byron, translated an article on Charles Babbage's Analytical Engine in 1843 and added extensive notes.","Her Note G describes an algorithm for calculating Bernoulli numbers, often called the first computer program. She foresaw machines creating music."],
"Irish Famine":["From 1845 potato blight destroyed the crop that most Irish poor relied on. Food continued to be exported from Ireland.","About a million died and a million emigrated. In Irish it is An Gorta Mór, the Great Hunger."],
"Italy unified":["In 1860 Giuseppe Garibaldi and his Thousand Redshirts conquered Sicily and Naples, then handed them to Victor Emmanuel II of Piedmont.","The Kingdom of Italy was proclaimed in 1861. Rome was added in 1870."],
"Germ theory":["In the 1860s Louis Pasteur showed, with swan-necked flasks that let in air but not dust, that broth stayed sterile unless microbes reached it: life does not arise spontaneously. He found microbes caused wine and milk to spoil, and invented pasteurization.", "Robert Koch in Germany identified the bacteria behind anthrax (1876), tuberculosis (1882) and cholera (1883). Germ theory transformed medicine, hygiene and public health."],
"Mendel's genetics":["Gregor Mendel, an Augustinian friar in Brno, bred some 28,000 pea plants in the monastery garden between 1856 and 1863, tracking traits like flower colour and seed shape.", "He found they passed on in predictable ratios, carried by paired 'factors' that we now call genes. He published in 1866, but was ignored until three scientists rediscovered his work in 1900."],
"Antiseptic surgery":["Before the 1860s, nearly half of patients who had limbs amputated in some hospitals died of infection. Joseph Lister, in Glasgow, read Pasteur and reasoned that germs caused the rot.", "In 1867 he reported using carbolic acid to clean wounds, dressings and instruments, and deaths fell sharply. Surgeons began washing their hands; Ignaz Semmelweis had urged the same in Vienna years before, and been ignored."],
"Germany unified":["Otto von Bismarck, Prussia's chancellor, united Germany through wars with Denmark, Austria and finally France, which Prussia and its German allies crushed in 1870.", "On 18 January 1871, in the Hall of Mirrors at Versailles, Wilhelm I of Prussia was proclaimed German emperor. France was forced to give up Alsace-Lorraine, a bitterness that fed later wars."],
"Impressionism":["In 1874, painters repeatedly rejected by the official Paris Salon, among them Monet, Renoir, Degas, Pissarro and Berthe Morisot, held their own exhibition in the photographer Nadar's former studio.", "A critic mocked Monet's Impression, Sunrise, calling them all 'Impressionists'; they adopted the name. They painted outdoors, in quick brushstrokes, capturing light and modern life."]
});
