window.STORY6=Object.assign(window.STORY6||{},{
"Maykop & Kura-Araxes":["In 1897 an archaeologist opened a great kurgan at Maykop in the northern Caucasus and found a chieftain buried under a canopy held up by silver and gold rods, with gold bulls, lions and vessels, around 3500 BCE.","South of the mountains, the Kura-Araxes culture spread across today's Armenia, Georgia and Azerbaijan, with round houses and distinctive black polished pottery. The region was an early centre of metalworking."],
"Andronovo culture":["From about 1800 to 900 BCE, herders of the Andronovo culture spread across the Kazakh steppe and southern Siberia, living in villages, mining copper and tin, and driving chariots.","Many scholars think they spoke early Indo-Iranian languages, the ancestors of Persian and Sanskrit. Their burials and fire altars echo customs in the Avesta and Rigveda."],
"Deer stones":["From about 1200 to 600 BCE, people in Mongolia and southern Siberia carved hundreds of standing stones with flying deer whose antlers sweep back along their bodies, along with belts, weapons and earrings.","The stones seem to represent warriors or chieftains. They often stand beside khirigsuurs, stone mounds ringed with the buried skulls of horses."],
"Scythians":["From around 700 BCE, Scythian horse-riding nomads ranged from the Black Sea to Siberia. They were superb archers and craftsmen in gold, making pectorals and combs covered with animals.","Herodotus described their customs, including inhaling hemp smoke in tents. Their own story survives only through Greeks and their tombs."],
"Targitai, first Scythian":["Herodotus heard from the Scythians that their first man was Targitai, son of Zeus and a daughter of the river Borysthenes, the Dnieper. His three sons saw a golden plough, yoke, battle-axe and cup fall from the sky.","When the older brothers approached, the gold burst into flame; it cooled only for the youngest, Kolaxais, who became king. The Scythians kept the gold as sacred."],
"Colchis":["Colchis, on Georgia's Black Sea coast, was a rich kingdom from around 600 BCE, with gold work and trade with Greek colonies.","Greeks set the myth of Jason and the Golden Fleece there. Georgians see it as an early part of their history."],
"Pazyryk tombs":["In the Altai mountains of Siberia, nomad chiefs were buried around 400 BCE in log chambers under stone mounds. Water seeped in and froze, preserving everything.","Archaeologists found tattooed bodies, felt hangings, saddles, horses with masks, and the oldest known pile carpet. In 1993, a tattooed woman, the 'Ice Maiden', was found."],
"Sarmatians":["From around 300 BCE the Sarmatians, Iranian-speaking nomads, displaced the Scythians on the steppes north of the Black Sea.","Women were buried with weapons; perhaps a fifth of warrior graves are of women, which may lie behind Greek tales of Amazons."],
"Xiongnu drive out the Yuezhi":["Around 176 BCE the Xiongnu defeated the Yuezhi, who lived in the Gansu corridor. The Xiongnu ruler made a drinking cup from the Yuezhi king's skull.","The Yuezhi migrated west to Bactria, where Zhang Qian found them in 129 BCE. One of their clans later founded the Kushan Empire."],
"Xianbei replace the Xiongnu":["Around 155 CE, the Xianbei leader Tanshihuai united nomad tribes and drove out the remaining Xiongnu from the Mongolian steppe.","Xianbei groups later ruled northern China, including the Northern Wei dynasty, which built the Yungang Buddhist caves."],
"Goths on the Black Sea":["By around 230 CE, Goths, a Germanic people, had moved from the Baltic to the lands north of the Black Sea in today's Ukraine.","They raided the Roman Empire by land and sea. The Gothic bishop Ulfilas translated the Bible into Gothic in the 300s, inventing an alphabet."],
"Khazar Khaganate":["From around 650 the Khazars, a Turkic people, ruled the steppes between the Volga, the Caucasus and the Black Sea, controlling trade and holding off Arab armies.","Around the 8th or 9th century their ruling elite adopted Judaism. A letter from the Khazar king Joseph to a Jewish scholar in Córdoba tells the conversion story."],
"Samanid Bukhara":["The Samanids, a Persian dynasty, ruled Central Asia from Bukhara from 819 to 999.","They revived Persian language and literature; the poet Rudaki wrote at their court. Their mausoleum in Bukhara is a masterpiece of brickwork."],
"Olga's revenge":["The Primary Chronicle says that in 945 the Drevlians killed Igor, prince of Kyiv, and proposed that his widow Olga marry their prince.","She buried their envoys alive and burned others in a bathhouse. Then she demanded three pigeons and a sparrow from each house, tied burning sulphur to them, and released them to set the town ablaze. Olga later became a Christian and a saint."],
"Perun & the pagan gods":["The Primary Chronicle says that in 980 Prince Vladimir set up statues of pagan gods on a hill in Kyiv: Perun, god of thunder, with a silver head and a gold moustache, along with Khors, Dazhbog, Stribog, Simargl and Mokosh.","After his baptism in 988, Vladimir had Perun's statue dragged to the Dnieper and thrown in."],
"Avicenna's Canon of Medicine":["Ibn Sina, Avicenna, born near Bukhara, said he had read all of medicine by sixteen. His Canon of Medicine, completed around 1025, organized all known medical knowledge.","Translated into Latin, it was a textbook in European universities into the 1600s. He also wrote on philosophy, physics and astronomy."],
"Al-Biruni":["Abu Rayhan al-Biruni, from Khwarazm, measured the Earth's radius from a mountain in today's Pakistan, within about 1 percent.","He accompanied Mahmud of Ghazni to India, learned Sanskrit, and wrote a careful, respectful study of Indian religion and science."],
"Battle of Didgori":["On 12 August 1121, King David IV of Georgia, 'the Builder', defeated a much larger Seljuk army at Didgori.","He took Tbilisi in 1122. Georgia entered a golden age."],
"Queen Tamar of Georgia":["Tamar became Georgia's ruler in 1184, and was crowned 'king'. Under her, Georgia reached its greatest extent and wealth.","Georgian chroniclers praised her wisdom and piety; the Georgian Orthodox Church honours her as a saint."]
});
// Full stories: Russia & Steppe, part 2.
window.STORY6=Object.assign(window.STORY6||{},{
"The Tale of Igor's Campaign":["In 1185 Prince Igor of Novgorod-Seversky led a raid against the Cumans, Turkic nomads of the steppe. His army was destroyed and he was captured, though he later escaped.","The Tale of Igor's Campaign turns the defeat into a lament calling the princes of Rus' to unite. Its only manuscript burned in Moscow's fire of 1812; Borodin based his opera Prince Igor on it."],
"The Knight in the Panther's Skin":["Shota Rustaveli, at Queen Tamar's court around 1200, wrote this epic of two knights, Avtandil and Tariel, who search for Tariel's lost love, the princess Nestan-Darejan.","It praises friendship, loyalty and love between equals. Georgian brides once brought a copy as part of their dowry."],
"The blue wolf & the doe":["The Secret History of the Mongols opens: 'There was a blue-grey wolf, born with his destiny ordained by Heaven above. His wife was a fallow doe'. From them descended the Mongol clans and Genghis Khan.","Written for the Mongol court around 1240, it is the oldest work of Mongolian literature, telling the story of Temüjin's rise from the inside."],
"Battle on the Ice":["In April 1242, Prince Alexander Nevsky of Novgorod defeated the Livonian Order of crusading knights near Lake Peipus.","Later legend says the knights crashed through the ice. Eisenstein's 1938 film made it a patriotic epic. The Orthodox Church made Alexander a saint."],
"Rise of Moscow":["Moscow was a minor town until its princes won favour with the Golden Horde. Ivan I, 'Kalita', 'Moneybag', collected tribute for the khan.","In 1325 the metropolitan, head of the Russian Church, moved to Moscow, making it the spiritual centre."],
"Battle of Kulikovo":["On 8 September 1380, Grand Prince Dmitry of Moscow defeated the army of the Golden Horde's general Mamai at Kulikovo field, near the Don.","He was called Dmitry Donskoy. Two years later, the Horde burned Moscow, but Russians see Kulikovo as a turning point."],
"Rublev's Trinity icon":["Around 1411, the monk Andrei Rublev painted the Trinity icon for the Trinity monastery, showing the three angels who visited Abraham, seated around a table.","Its soft colours and harmony made it the most famous Russian icon. Tarkovsky made a film about Rublev."],
"Ulugh Beg's observatory":["Ulugh Beg, Timur's grandson and ruler of Samarkand, was an astronomer. In 1420 he built an observatory with a huge sextant cut into a hill.","His star catalogue of over 1,000 stars was the most accurate since Ptolemy. He was murdered in 1449 on his son's orders."],
"Book of Dede Korkut":["The Book of Dede Korkut, written down around the 1400s or 1500s, preserves twelve stories of the Oghuz Turks, told by the wise bard Dede Korkut.","In one, a hero fights a one-eyed giant, like the Cyclops. Azerbaijan, Turkey and Turkmenistan all claim it as their heritage."],
"Moscow takes Novgorod":["Novgorod was a rich merchant republic, trading furs with the Hanseatic League, governed by its assembly, the veche, called together by a bell. Birch-bark letters written by ordinary Novgorodians, found since 1951, show a remarkably literate city.", "In 1478 Ivan III of Moscow annexed it, deported leading families, and carried off the veche bell to Moscow."],
"End of the Mongol yoke":["In 1480 Ivan III stopped paying tribute to the Great Horde. Khan Akhmat marched against him.","The armies faced each other across the Ugra River for weeks, the 'Great Stand'. Akhmat withdrew, ending over two centuries of Mongol overlordship."],
"Ivan the Terrible, first tsar":["In 1547 Ivan IV was crowned the first 'tsar of all Russia', from the Latin Caesar.","He conquered Kazan and Astrakhan, but his secret police, the oprichniki, terrorized the country. He killed his own son in a rage in 1581."],
"Conquest of Kazan":["In 1552, Ivan IV's army besieged and stormed Kazan, capital of a Tatar khanate on the Volga, using mines to blow up its walls.","Tatar Muslims were massacred or expelled from the city. Russia expanded east along the Volga. Tatars remember Queen Söyembikä."],
"St Basil's Cathedral":["Ivan IV built St Basil's Cathedral on Red Square from 1555 to celebrate the capture of Kazan.","Its colourful onion domes represent the flames of a bonfire. Legend says Ivan blinded its architect, but there is no evidence."],
"Yermak enters Siberia":["In 1582 the Cossack Yermak Timofeyevich, hired by the Stroganov merchant family, crossed the Urals with a few hundred men and took Qashliq, capital of the Siberian khanate.","Yermak drowned in 1585. Russian conquest spread across Siberia, taking fur tribute from Indigenous peoples."],
"Romanov dynasty":["In 1613, after the Time of Troubles, an assembly elected sixteen-year-old Michael Romanov as tsar.","Legend says the peasant Ivan Susanin led Polish troops astray into a forest to save him. The Romanovs ruled until 1917."],
"Russians reach the Pacific":["In 1639 a small Cossack party under Ivan Moskvitin, following rivers and portages from Yakutsk, reached the Sea of Okhotsk, the first Russians to stand on the Pacific coast.", "Russia's expansion from the Urals to the Pacific took less than sixty years, driven by the hunt for sable furs. Indigenous peoples such as the Yakuts (Sakha), Evenks and Chukchi were forced to pay yasak, fur tribute; the Chukchi resisted for over a century."],
"Old Believers' schism":["In the 1650s Patriarch Nikon changed Russian Orthodox rituals, such as making the sign of the cross with three fingers instead of two.","Those who refused, the Old Believers, were persecuted. Some burned themselves rather than submit. The archpriest Avvakum wrote his autobiography before being burned."],
"Stenka Razin's revolt":["In 1670 the Don Cossack Stepan 'Stenka' Razin, already famous for raiding Persia's Caspian coast, led a rebellion of Cossacks, peasants and Volga peoples, taking Astrakhan, Tsaritsyn and Samara, and promising freedom from landlords and officials.", "He was defeated at Simbirsk, betrayed, and quartered in Red Square in 1671. The folk song 'From beyond the island to the river's reach' remembers him throwing a Persian princess into the Volga."]
});
// Full stories: Russia & Steppe, part 3.
window.STORY6=Object.assign(window.STORY6||{},{
"Treaty of Nerchinsk":["Russian Cossacks pushing into the Amur valley clashed with Qing China, which besieged their fort at Albazin. In 1689 the two empires met at Nerchinsk.","Jesuit priests in Qing service, Jean-François Gerbillon and Tomás Pereira, translated, and the treaty was written in Latin, Russian, Manchu and Chinese. Russia gave up the Amur and gained trade rights. It was China's first treaty with a European state on equal terms."],
"Great Northern War":["In 1700 Peter the Great joined Denmark and Saxony against Sweden. The young Swedish king Charles XII crushed the Russians at Narva.","Peter rebuilt his army, and in 1709 destroyed Charles's army at Poltava in Ukraine; the Cossack hetman Ivan Mazepa had joined the Swedes. The 1721 Treaty of Nystad gave Russia the Baltic coast."],
"St Petersburg founded":["In May 1703 Peter the Great founded St Petersburg on marshland at the mouth of the Neva, taken from Sweden. Tens of thousands of forced labourers and serfs, many of whom died, built it.","He made it his capital, his 'window on Europe', forcing nobles to move there, shave their beards and wear European dress."],
"Bering reaches Alaska":["Vitus Bering, a Dane in Russian service, led the Great Northern Expedition. In July 1741 his ship sighted Mount Saint Elias in Alaska; the naturalist Georg Steller landed on Kayak Island for ten hours.","On the return, the ship was wrecked on an island, where Bering and many crew died of scurvy. Survivors built a boat from the wreck. Russian fur traders soon followed, exploiting the Aleut and Alutiiq peoples."],
"Moscow University":["Mikhail Lomonosov, a fisherman's son from the White Sea who walked to Moscow to study, became a chemist, physicist, poet and grammarian.","In 1755, with Count Ivan Shuvalov, he founded Moscow University, open to all free classes. It now bears his name."],
"Dzungar genocide":["The Dzungars, the last great nomad empire, ruled western Mongolia and Xinjiang. From 1755, the Qianlong Emperor's armies destroyed them.","Massacres, smallpox and flight killed perhaps 70 to 80 percent of the Dzungars, maybe half a million or more people. Historians describe it as a genocide."],
"Catherine the Great":["In 1762 Catherine, a German princess, overthrew her husband Peter III in a coup with the guards; he was killed days later.","She corresponded with Voltaire and Diderot, expanded Russia to the Black Sea and took much of Poland. Yet she tightened serfdom. She ruled 34 years."],
"Pugachev's Rebellion":["In 1773 the Don Cossack Yemelyan Pugachev claimed to be the dead tsar Peter III and raised a vast rebellion of Cossacks, serfs, factory workers, Bashkirs and Tatars.","They killed landlords and took Kazan. Pugachev was captured and executed in 1775. Pushkin wrote a history of the revolt and the novel The Captain's Daughter."],
"Russia annexes the Crimean Khanate":["The Crimean Khanate, ruled by descendants of Genghis Khan, had been an Ottoman ally for three centuries. In 1783 Catherine the Great annexed it.","Many Crimean Tatars emigrated to the Ottoman Empire. Prince Potemkin founded Sevastopol as a naval base. Legend says he built fake 'Potemkin villages' for Catherine's tour."],
"Russia annexes Georgia":["In 1783 the eastern Georgian kingdom of Kartli-Kakheti accepted Russian protection. In 1795 Persia sacked Tbilisi.","In 1801 Russia annexed the kingdom outright, abolishing the Georgian monarchy. The Georgian Church's independence was ended in 1811."],
"Napoleon invades Russia":["In June 1812 Napoleon crossed the Neman with over 600,000 men. The Russians retreated, burning crops, and fought at Borodino.","Napoleon entered Moscow, which burned. In the winter retreat, cold, hunger and Cossack attacks destroyed his army. Tolstoy's War and Peace tells the story."],
"Decembrist revolt":["In December 1825, liberal army officers, many of whom had seen Europe during the Napoleonic wars, led troops onto Senate Square in St Petersburg, demanding a constitution.","Nicholas I crushed them with cannon. Five leaders were hanged and others exiled to Siberia; their wives followed them."],
"Pushkin":["Alexander Pushkin, whose great-grandfather Abram Gannibal was an African enslaved as a child and raised by Peter the Great, became Russia's national poet.","He wrote Eugene Onegin and Boris Godunov. He died in a duel in 1837, aged 37."],
"Imam Shamil's resistance":["From 1834, Imam Shamil, an Avar from Dagestan, led Chechens and Dagestanis in a holy war against Russian conquest of the North Caucasus.","He held out for 25 years before surrendering in 1859. Tolstoy's Hadji Murat describes the war."],
"Crimean War":["From 1853 to 1856, Russia fought the Ottoman Empire, Britain, France and Sardinia, sparked by a dispute over Christian holy places and Russian ambitions in the Ottoman lands. The allies besieged Sevastopol for nearly a year.", "More soldiers died of disease than in battle. Florence Nightingale and the Jamaican-born Mary Seacole cared for the wounded, and Leo Tolstoy, an artillery officer, wrote the Sevastopol Sketches. Russia's defeat pushed Alexander II toward reform."],
"Baba Yaga & Koschei":["Between 1855 and 1863, Alexander Afanasyev published hundreds of Russian folk tales. In them, Baba Yaga is a bony-legged witch who lives deep in the forest in a hut on chicken legs and flies in a mortar, sometimes devouring visitors, sometimes helping them.", "Koschei the Deathless, a sorcerer who kidnaps princesses, cannot be killed because his death is hidden: in a needle, inside an egg, inside a duck, inside a hare, in an iron chest buried under an oak on an island."]
});
