window.STORY6=Object.assign(window.STORY6||{},{
"Kim Suro of Gaya":["The Samguk Yusa records that in 42 CE, nine chiefs of the Gaya region heard a voice from Mount Gujibong telling them to sing and dance. A purple cord came down from the sky with a red cloth holding a golden box of six golden eggs.","Six boys hatched; the first, Kim Suro, became king of Geumgwan Gaya. He married Heo Hwang-ok, who the chronicle says sailed from the distant land of Ayuta, often identified with Ayodhya in India. Millions of Koreans named Kim and Heo trace their ancestry to them."],
"Yamato Takeru":["The Kojiki and Nihon Shoki tell of Prince Yamato Takeru, sent by his father to subdue rebels in the west and east. Trapped in a burning grass field, he cut the grass with the sacred sword Kusanagi, 'grass-cutter', and turned the fire back on his enemies.","He died on the way home and turned into a white bird. The sword is said to be kept at Atsuta Shrine, one of Japan's three imperial regalia."],
"Zhang Heng's seismoscope":["In 132 CE the Han astronomer Zhang Heng built a bronze vessel with eight dragons around it, each holding a ball over a waiting toad. When an earthquake struck, a ball dropped from the dragon facing the quake's direction.","Court records say it once detected an earthquake no one in the capital felt; days later a messenger confirmed one in Gansu. Zhang Heng also made a water-powered armillary sphere."],
"Daoist church founded":["In 142 CE, Zhang Daoling said Laozi appeared to him on Mount Heming in Sichuan and gave him authority as Celestial Master.","His movement, the Way of the Celestial Masters, healed the sick through confession and charged five pecks of rice as dues. His grandson set up a theocratic state in Hanzhong. The Celestial Masters lineage survives in Taiwan."],
"Yellow Turban Rebellion":["In 184 CE, Zhang Jue, a Daoist healer, led hundreds of thousands of peasants in revolt against the Han, wearing yellow headscarves. They proclaimed that 'the Blue Heaven is dead; the Yellow Heaven will rise'.","The revolt was crushed, but the warlords who fought it became more powerful than the emperor, leading to the fall of the Han."],
"Oath of the Peach Garden":["The 14th-century novel Romance of the Three Kingdoms opens with three men, Liu Bei, a distant royal relative; Guan Yu, a fugitive; and Zhang Fei, a butcher, swearing brotherhood in a peach garden to fight the Yellow Turbans.","They vowed to die on the same day. Guan Yu became a god of loyalty and war, worshipped in temples across East Asia."],
"Empress Jingū":["The Nihon Shoki says Empress Jingū, after her husband died, led an army to Korea while pregnant, delaying birth by tying stones to her belly. The Korean kingdoms submitted.","Historians treat her as legendary; Korean chronicles record nothing similar."],
"Porcelain":["By the Eastern Han dynasty, potters in Zhejiang were firing stoneware with a glassy glaze at over 1,200 °C, an early form of porcelain, called celadon for its green colour.", "Pure white porcelain was perfected later, and the kilns of Jingdezhen made blue-and-white ware under the Yuan and Ming, exported to the Middle East, Africa and Europe. Europeans could not make porcelain until 1708 in Saxony; in English, it is still called 'china'."],
"Battle of Red Cliffs":["In the winter of 208, the northern warlord Cao Cao led a huge fleet down the Yangtze. Liu Bei and Sun Quan's allied forces set fire to his ships at Red Cliffs.","Cao Cao retreated, and China was split into three kingdoms. The novel and John Woo's film made it famous."],
"Han dynasty falls":["In 220 Cao Pi, son of the warlord Cao Cao, forced the last Han emperor, Xian, a puppet since childhood, to abdicate, and declared himself emperor of Wei. Xian lived out his life peacefully as Duke of Shanyang.", "Liu Bei declared the Shu Han dynasty in Sichuan, and Sun Quan the Wu dynasty in the south, beginning the Three Kingdoms. The 400-year Han dynasty was over, and China was divided for over three centuries."],
"Queen Himiko":["Chinese records say that in 239 Himiko, queen of Wa (Japan), sent envoys to the Wei court. She was a shaman, unmarried, who ruled through 'the way of demons', with a thousand women attendants.","The Wei emperor gave her a golden seal and bronze mirrors. Where her land, Yamatai, was is debated."],
"Kofun Japan":["From around 250, rulers in Japan were buried in giant mounds, kofun, many keyhole-shaped, surrounded by moats and lined with clay figures called haniwa: warriors, horses, houses and dancers.", "The largest, the Daisen Kofun near Osaka, attributed to Emperor Nintoku, is about 486 metres long, one of the largest tombs in the world. The Imperial Household Agency restricts excavation of royal tombs."],
"Wang Xizhi's calligraphy":["Wang Xizhi, the 'Sage of Calligraphy', wrote the Preface to the Orchid Pavilion poems in 353 at a drinking party where cups floated down a stream.","Emperor Taizong of Tang loved it so much he had the original buried with him."],
"Zu Chongzhi's pi":["Around 480, the mathematician and astronomer Zu Chongzhi, working with his son Zu Gengzhi, calculated pi to between 3.1415926 and 3.1415927, likely by inscribing polygons with over 12,000 sides in a circle.", "No more accurate value was found for about 900 years. He also gave 355/113 as a close fraction and designed a calendar, the Daming calendar, that improved on earlier ones."],
"Hua Mulan":["The Ballad of Mulan, a folk poem from the Northern Wei era around the 500s, tells of a girl weaving at her loom who sighs because her old father has been called up to fight. She buys a horse and saddle and goes in his place, disguised as a man.", "She fights for twelve years. Offered a high post by the emperor, she asks only for a camel to ride home, puts on her old clothes and makeup, and her comrades are astonished. The ballad ends: who can tell the male hare from the female when they run side by side?"],
"Sui reunites China":["In 581 Yang Jian, a northern general, founded the Sui dynasty, and in 589 his armies crossed the Yangtze and conquered the southern Chen dynasty, reuniting China after nearly three centuries of division.", "As Emperor Wen, he reformed the law, taxes and land distribution, and built granaries. His son Yang Guang, Emperor Yang, launched huge projects and disastrous wars against Goguryeo, and the dynasty fell after just 37 years."]
});
