"Macassan trepangers":["From around 1720 or earlier, Makassarese sailors from Sulawesi sailed to northern Australia each year on the monsoon winds to gather trepang, sea cucumbers, to sell to China.","They traded cloth, tobacco, metal and dugout canoes with Yolŋu people, and some Yolŋu sailed back with them to Makassar. Yolŋu languages contain Makassarese words, and their art depicts the praus."],
"Konbaung dynasty":["In 1752 Alaungpaya, a village headman, founded the Konbaung dynasty and reunited Burma. His successors sacked Ayutthaya in 1767 and repelled four Qing invasions.","Burma fought three wars with Britain, losing its last king, Thibaw, in 1885, exiled to India."],
"Europeans reach Tahiti":["In June 1767 the British ship Dolphin under Samuel Wallis reached Tahiti. After a clash in which British guns killed many Tahitians, trade followed; Purea, a high-ranking woman, befriended Wallis.","The French under Bougainville came in 1768 and described Tahiti as a paradise. Visiting ships brought disease."],
"Cook in the Pacific":["James Cook's three voyages from 1768 to 1779 charted New Zealand, eastern Australia and Hawaiʻi. The Tahitian navigator and priest Tupaia joined the first voyage and drew a map of over 70 islands.","In 1770 Gweagal people at Botany Bay met Cook's landing with spears. Cook was killed at Kealakekua Bay in Hawaiʻi in 1779 after trying to take the chief Kalaniʻōpuʻu hostage."],
"Tây Sơn rebellion":["In 1771 three brothers from the village of Tây Sơn in central Vietnam rose against the Nguyễn lords, promising to help the poor.","Nguyễn Huệ defeated Siamese and Qing invasions, taking Hanoi in a lightning campaign at Tết 1789, and became Emperor Quang Trung. The Nguyễn dynasty defeated the Tây Sơn in 1802."],
"Bangkok founded":["After Ayutthaya fell in 1767, General Taksin rebuilt Siam from Thonburi. In 1782 General Chakri took power as Rama I and moved the capital across the river to Bangkok.","Its full Thai name is the longest place name in the world. The Chakri dynasty still reigns."],
"Pele":["In Hawaiian tradition, Pele is the goddess of volcanoes and fire, who travelled from Kahiki and now lives in Halemaʻumaʻu crater at Kīlauea. Her chants are part of hula.","In 1790 an explosive eruption killed many warriors of Keōua, a rival of Kamehameha; their footprints survive in the ash. Many saw it as Pele favouring Kamehameha."],
"Musket Wars":["From around 1807 to the 1840s, Māori iwi fought one another using muskets obtained from Europeans. Ngāpuhi under Hongi Hika, who visited England and met King George IV, led raids.","Perhaps 20,000 died and many were enslaved or displaced. The wars reshaped tribal territories."],
"Kingdom of Hawaiʻi unified":["Kamehameha I, a chief from Hawaiʻi Island, fought for years to unite the islands, using Western cannon and advisers. At the battle of Nuʻuanu on Oʻahu in 1795, his warriors drove the defenders over a cliff. In 1810 King Kaumualiʻi of Kauaʻi agreed to submit peacefully.", "Kamehameha's 'Law of the Splintered Paddle', Kānāwai Māmalahoe, protected the weak, such as elders and children, from harm on the roads; it is in Hawaiʻi's state constitution. He died in 1819, and his queen Kaʻahumanu soon ended the old kapu system."],
"Raffles at Singapore":["In January 1819 Stamford Raffles of the East India Company landed at Singapore, where a Malay settlement and the Temenggong of Johor lived. To get a legal treaty, he recognized Hussein Shah, an elder prince passed over for the Johor throne, as sultan.", "Singapore became a free port and grew fast, attracting Chinese, Malay, Indian and Bugis traders. The Dutch protested, but recognized it in 1824. Singapore marked its bicentennial in 2019 with an emphasis on its 700-year history before Raffles."]
