"Australian Federation":["On 1 January 1901, six British colonies united as the Commonwealth of Australia, after referendums. Edmund Barton became the first prime minister.","One of the first laws, the Immigration Restriction Act, began the 'White Australia' policy, which lasted until the 1970s. The Constitution excluded Aboriginal people from the census until a 1967 referendum."],
"Bikini Atoll nuclear tests":["In 1946 the US asked the 167 people of Bikini Atoll in the Marshall Islands to leave 'for the good of mankind'. From 1946 to 1958 the US tested 67 nuclear weapons in the Marshall Islands.","The 1954 Castle Bravo test, far stronger than expected, showered radioactive fallout on Rongelap and on a Japanese fishing boat. Bikini remains largely uninhabited; Marshallese still seek justice for health effects."],
"Indonesian mass killings":["After a murky coup attempt on 30 September 1965 that killed six generals, the army under Suharto blamed the Communist Party, PKI. Over the next months, the army and civilian militias killed perhaps 500,000 to a million people.","Hundreds of thousands were jailed for years. Suharto took power, ruling until 1998. Joshua Oppenheimer's film The Act of Killing showed perpetrators boasting of the killings."],
"ASEAN founded":["On 8 August 1967, foreign ministers of Indonesia, Malaysia, the Philippines, Singapore and Thailand signed the Bangkok Declaration, founding the Association of Southeast Asian Nations, partly to ease regional tensions after Indonesia's confrontation with Malaysia.", "It grew to ten members by 1999, and eleven when Timor-Leste joined in October 2025. Its 'ASEAN way' of consensus and non-interference has kept peace among members, but critics say it has done little about abuses, as in Myanmar."],
"Fall of Saigon":["On 30 April 1975, North Vietnamese tanks broke through the gates of the Presidential Palace in Saigon. US helicopters had evacuated the last Americans and some Vietnamese from the embassy roof.","Vietnam was reunified, and Saigon renamed Ho Chi Minh City. Hundreds of thousands of South Vietnamese were sent to re-education camps, and over a million 'boat people' fled."],
"Cambodian genocide":["On 17 April 1975 the Khmer Rouge under Pol Pot took Phnom Penh and emptied the cities, declaring 'Year Zero'.","In under four years, perhaps 1.5 to 2 million people died from execution, starvation and forced labour. At Tuol Sleng prison, S-21, about 14,000 were tortured; only a handful survived. Vietnam invaded in 1979."],
"People Power":["In February 1986, after Ferdinand Marcos claimed victory in a fraudulent election over Corazon Aquino, widow of the murdered opposition leader Benigno Aquino, millions of Filipinos filled EDSA avenue in Manila.","Nuns and civilians faced down tanks. Marcos fled to Hawaiʻi, and Aquino became president."],
"Timor-Leste independent":["Portugal left East Timor in 1975, and Indonesia invaded days after it declared independence. Perhaps 100,000 to 180,000 died under occupation.","In a UN-run vote in 1999, 78.5 percent chose independence. Militias destroyed much of the country. Timor-Leste became independent on 20 May 2002."],
"Indian Ocean tsunami":["On 26 December 2004, a magnitude 9.1 earthquake off Sumatra caused tsunamis that struck coasts across the Indian Ocean.","About 230,000 people died in 14 countries, most in Aceh. On Simeulue island, an oral tradition of 'smong' told people to run uphill; few died."],
"Apology to the Stolen Generations":["On 13 February 2008, Prime Minister Kevin Rudd stood in parliament and apologized to the Stolen Generations: Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander children removed from their families by governments and churches, from the early 1900s to the 1970s, often to be raised in institutions or white families.", "The 1997 report Bringing Them Home, based on hundreds of testimonies, estimated between one in three and one in ten Aboriginal children were taken. Crowds gathered to watch the apology on screens across the country, many in tears."],
"Myanmar earthquake":["On 28 March 2025, a magnitude 7.7 earthquake ruptured the Sagaing Fault, with its epicentre near Mandalay, Myanmar's second city. The shaking toppled monasteries and apartment blocks, and was felt as far as Bangkok, where a building under construction collapsed.", "At least 3,800 people died. Myanmar was already torn by civil war since the military's 2021 coup, and the junta kept up air strikes in the days after, hampering relief."]
